The members of the Wales Assembly are worried that they "look like muppets"
Some of the members of the Assembly are stunned by the proposal to change their title to “CHUP” (members of the Welsh parliament).
This proposal is provoked by plans to change the name of the Assembly of the Parliament of Wales.
Members from across the political spectrum are worried that this could lead to ridicule.
One of Labour has said that his group is worried that the new name is “rhymed with Dumb and Conduce.”
For readers outside of Wales: In Welsh this definitely has an offensive meaning.
According to one of the party’s Plaid members, the whole group is “not happy” and has offered alternatives.
A representative of the Welsh conservative party has pointed out that his group is “not biased” to changing the name, but notes that the new name PMP has a certain verbal resemblance to “mump”.
In this context, the Welsh letter W is pronounced similarly to the English Yorkshire pronunciation of the letter U.
The Commission of the Assembly, which is currently drafting a draft of the bill to change the name, made the following statement: “Of course, the final decision on all the descriptive names by which the members of the Assembly are called will belong to the members themselves.”
The Wales Government Act of 2017 gave the Welsh Assembly the power to change its own name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposals, which established a broad support for the Assembly to start being called ‘Parliament of Wales’.
As for the post of its members, the Commission prefers ‘Members of Parliament of Wales, in short ‘CHPD’, but the ‘CHUP’ option has received the most support following a public consultation.
Members of the Assembly are proposing alternative options, but reaching a consensus will clearly be a headache for President Elin Jones, from whom he is expected to submit a draft of the bill for the change within a few weeks.
The reformist bill will include other changes in the way the Assembly operates, including rules for disqualifying its members, as well as the very design of the commission system.
The members of the Assembly will have the final vote on how they will be called when they come to a discussion on the bill.
The Macedonians will vote in a referendum on whether to change the name of their country
On Sunday, voters will vote on whether to change the name of their country to the “Republic of North Macedonia”.
The referendum is planned to resolve a decades-long dispute with neighboring Greece, in which there is an administrative area called “Macedonia”.
Athens has long insisted that its northern neighbor's name represents a claim to Greek state territory, and has repeatedly objected to its attempts to join the EU and NATO.
Macedonian President Georgi Ivanov, who is an opponent in the referendum on changing the name of the state, has ruled that he will not respect the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that changing the country's name is simply the price to be paid to join the EU and NATO.
St. Martin's bells fall silent amid difficulties at Harlem churches
“If we look historically, according to the old people I talked to, there was a bar and a church literally on every corner,” he said.
“Both have disappeared today.”
Adams says the disappearance of the bars is understandable.
“People today are socializing in different ways,” he added.
“Bars are no longer neighborhood living rooms where everyone gathers regularly.”
As for the churches, he expresses the concern that the money from the sale of the assets will not last as long as the executives expect, “and sooner or later they will be in the same situation.”
The man adds that churches can be replaced by blocks of apartments where people live that don't help survive the neighborhood's remaining sanctuaries.
“The vast majority of people who buy housing in these buildings will be white, which will speed up the process of closing these churches, as it is not particularly likely that most of the new settlers will visit them.”
Both churches were built from white pastures before Harlem became a neighborhood of the Blacks – the Metropolitan Community in 1870, and St. Martyr a decade later.
The original white Methodist congregation moved out in the years after 1930.
The building then became the possession of the neighborhood's black residents.
“St. Martin” was taken over by black Christians under the direction of Reverend John Howard Johnson, who staged a boycot against retailers on 125th Street, which is Harlem’s main market street because they didn’t want to hire black employees.
In 1939, the building suffered heavy damage in a fire, but Father Johnson's parishioners had drawn up a plan for its restoration, ordering the bells.
Reverend David Johnson, Father Johnson’s son and successor to “St. Martin,” proudly called them “the poor bells.”
The expert, who used the bells in July, has another description: “Cultural Treasure” and “indispensable historical instrument.”
The relevant expert, Tiffany Ngu of the University of Michigan, also noted that this was the first bell system in the world to have been used by a black musician – Dionysio A. Lind, who transferred to the larger system at Riverside Church 18 years ago.
Mr. Meryweather mentions that St. Martin has never been replaced.
What has played out in “St. Martin” over the past few weeks has been an intricate fairy tale woven by architects and performers, some brought by the secular leaders of the church and others by an Episcopal diocese.
From a vestiaria – which is the governing body of the flock, consisting of secular leaders – wrote to the diocese in July, expressing concerns that the diocese “would be willing to transfer costs to a vestiaria,” although he had no role in recruiting the architects and contractors sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners have complained about a lack of transparency from the diocese.
A 13-year-old has been bruised by a shark in a lobster hunt in California
A shark attacked and injured a 13-year-old boy last Saturday while it dived for lobsters in California on the first day of the hunting season, representative authorities said.
The attack happened shortly before 7 a.m. near Beacon Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Haml told KSWB-TV in San Diego that he was diving with friends about half an o'clock Saturday morning when he overheard the boy screaming for help and rushed along with an entire group to pull him out of the water.
Hammell first thought the child had caught lobster and got excited, then realized screaming “You bit me!
He bited me!”
Hammell said that when he got to the boy, he found out that the whole area of his collarbone had been torn apart.
“I’ve called everyone out of the water because there’s a shark!”
The boy was transported by helicopter to Radi Children’s Hospital in San Diego, where he was admitted in critical condition.
It is not yet known what kind of shark was responsible for the attack.
Saviour Captain Larry Jills said at a media briefing that a shark had been spotted in the water a few weeks earlier, but was judged not of a dangerous species.
Jills added that the victim suffered traumatic injuries to his upper body.
Responding authorities have closed access to the coastline from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Suomi in Encitinas for 48 hours for safety reasons while the investigation is ongoing.
Jills notes that there are more than 135 shark species in the area, most of which, however, do not pose a danger to humans.
Sainsbury is planning an offensive in the UK market for beauty products
Sainsbury confronts Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams by opening stands for beauty products in their establishments, where customers will be served by special assistants.
As part of the significant offensive in the beauty products market in the UK, which is valued at £2.8 billion and continues to grow, while sales of fashion and household products are declining, the largest beautifying product stands so far will be located in 11 stores across the country, after which, with the success of the initiative, such will be found in more outlets.
The investment turn in the beautification industry is being made at a time when supermarkets are looking for ways to use the vacant space on the racks, until recently occupied by televisions, microwave ovens and other home appliances.
Sainsbury has said it will double the number of beautifying products offered to 3000 products, for the first time including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
Current product ranges from L'Oréal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees will also get more space, similar to offerings in Boots stores.
The supermarket is also relaunching its boutique range of make-ups so that most products can be used by vegans – something increasingly sought after by younger consumers.
In addition, perfume retailer Fragrance Shop will trial discount programs at two Sainsbury stores, the first of which opened in Croyden, south London last week, while the second will be opened in Celly Oak, Birmingham later this year.
Shopping online and the shifting focus on shopping for smaller amounts of food on a daily basis at convenient times leads to supermarkets having to make more of an effort to attract shoppers.
Mike Cope, CEO of Sainsbury, has shared that the outlets will look more and more like department stores, while the supermarket chain is trying to fight discounters Aldi and Lidl by offering more services and non-food items.
Sainsbury is deploying Argos sites in hundreds of stores, also opening Habitats sites since they bought both chains two years ago – which the company says has boosted grocery sales and made acquisitions even more profitable.
The supermarket chain's previous attempt to reshape its beautification and pharmaceutical outlets was unsuccessful.
Sainsbury made a joint attempt with Boots at the beginning of the new century, but the association ended after a dispute over allocating profits from the pharmaceutical company's sites in supermarkets.
The new strategy was developed after Sainsbury sold its pharmaceutical business with 281 Celesio outlets, which owns the Lloyds Pharmacy chain, for £125 million three years ago.
From there, they claim Lloyds will have a role in the plan by providing a wide range of luxury skincare brands, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy in four stores.
Paul Mills-Hickes, Sainsbury’s commercial director, made the following statement: “We’ve transformed the type and character of our beautification stands to improve the overall experience of our customers.
We have also invested in special training of our colleagues, who will offer professional advice on site.
The range of brands we offer aims to meet every taste and everyone needed in a fascinating environment with a convenient location, and this combination makes us an attractive destination for beautiful products, which challenges the very concept of the old way of shopping.”
Peter Jones 'furious' after Holly Willobbie's refusal of £11 million deal
Dragons Den star Peter Jones "has become infuriated" since TV presenter Holly Willoughby has backtracked on an �£11million deal with his lifestyle company to focus on his new contracts with Marks and Spencer and ITV
Willoughby doesn't have time for the brand of household appliances and Truly accessories.
The couple's business has been compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
The 37-year-old presenter of "This Morning" has announced her departure on Instagram.
Holly Willoughby left Dragons Den star Peter Jones “boiling” after pulling out of their lucrative last-minute lifestyle products business – to focus on their own new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Sources said Jones “got angry” when TV’s “golden girl” confessed during a tense meeting on Tuesday at the headquarters of his business empire in Marlowe, Buckinghamshire, that her new contracts – worth up to £1.5 million – meant he would no longer have time for his brand of household products and Truly accessories.
The business has been likened to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand, with rumours that it has doubled Willoughby's fortune, the value of which is projected at £11million.
When 37-year-old Willoughbey announced on Instagram his departure to Truly, Jones left the UK on a plane to head for one of his holiday properties.
One of the sources shared the following: “Truly was definitely Holly’s top priority.
It was expected to be her long-term commitment to secure her future over the next few decades.
Her decision to leave boiled absolutely everyone completely unprepared.
No one believed this could happen on Tuesday, so close to the launch.
There’s a warehouse in Marlowe’s plant that’s full of waiting for products to be sold.”
Experts believe that the withdrawal of this Morning's presenter, which is one of Britain's most successful stars, could cost the company millions due to a large investment in products ranging from cushions and candles to clothing and household products, and could also lead to a delay in launching the brand.
And last but not least, it can also mean the end of a long friendship.
Wiloughby, who her mother of three, along with her husband Dan Baldwin has been a close friend of Jones and his wife Tara Kapp for ten years now.
Willoughby herself founded Truly along with Capp in 2016, as Jones, who is 52, had joined as the company's chairman in March.
The two families often go on holidays together, and Jones owns 40% of Baldwin's television production company.
Willobuy will become an “ambassador” of the M&S brand and will also replace Anne McParlyn as the host of “I Am a Celebrity” show on ITV.
A source close to Jones last night made the following statement: “We won’t comment on his business affairs.”
