Pig-murderer fatally injures Chinese farmer
A farmer was attacked and killed by a pig at a market in southwestern China, local media reported.
The man, identified only by his surname as Yuan, was found dead with an intermitted artery and covered in blood near a pigsty at the Liupanshui market in Guizhou province, the South China Morning Post reported on Sunday.
Pig breeder was preparing to inject his pigs with vaccines in May 30, 2005 in Xinin from Qinghai Province, China.
He allegedly arrived with his cousin from neighbouring Yunnan province on Wednesday to sell 15 pigs at the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead, also welding the door to the neighboring pigsty open.
He also reported that there was a large male pig in the pigsty with a bloodied zurla.
Forensic examination has confirmed that the 550-pound pig has fatally attacked the farmer, the report reported.
“My cousin’s legs were bloodied and disfigured,” his cousin Wu was quoted as saying by Guiyang Evening News.
Security camera footage shows Yuan going to the market at 4:40 a.m. to feed his pigs.
His body was discovered about an hour later.
The killer animal didn't belong to Yuan or his cousin.
The head of the market told Evening News that the pig had been locked up so he couldn't attack anyone else while police collected evidence from the scene.
It has also been reported that Yuan's family and market management are negotiating compensation for the death.
Although they are rare, it has previously been reported of pigs attacking humans.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on their farm in Massachusetts, and after the attack the man had critical injuries.
Ten years before that, a 650-point pig had nailed a Welsh farmer to his tractor until his wife managed to banish him.
Since an Oregon farmer was eaten by his own pigs in 2012, a Manitoba farmer told CBC News that the pigs were usually not aggressive, but the taste of the blood could affect them as a “trigger.”
“They’re just playing.
Pigs are like little children, extremely curious... they don't intend to hurt anyone.
But you also have to treat them with respect," the man concludes.
Weakening Hurricane Rosá will lead to frequent and torrential rains in the southwestern United States
Hurricane Rosá weakens as it moves over cool waters on the northern coast of Mexico.
However, in the coming days, Rosá will result in flooding rains in northern Mexico and southwestern U.S.
Rose includes winds with a strength of 85 miles per hour, a Category 1 hurricane as of 5 a.m. Sunday morning east and is located 385 miles southwest of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
On Sunday, Roza is expected to move north.
During this time, over the Pacific Ocean begins to form a synclinal, which moves eastwards to the west coast of the United States. As Rosa approaches the Baja California peninsula on Monday in the form of a tropical storm, it will begin to push tropical moisture deep north to the southwestern United States.
On Monday, Rosa will produce up to 10 inches of rain in some areas of Mexico.
Over the next few days thereafter, tropical moisture along with the approaching synclinal will create frequent heavy rains in the southwest.
Locally, 1 to 4 inches of rain will cause dangerous and rapid flooding, suffering debris streams and probable landslides in the desert.
Deep tropical moisture will lead to rainfall of 2 to 3 inches per hour in certain places, especially parts of southern Nevada and Arizona.
In some parts of the southwest, 2 to 4 inches of rain is expected, especially over most of Arizona.
There may be lightning floods in rapidly deteriorating conditions due to the dispersed nature of tropical rain.
It would be extremely imprudent to travel a handful in the desert when there is a threat of tropical rains.
Severe rain could turn canyons into turbulent rivers, and thunderstorms would lead to raised dust and gusty winds.
The approaching synclinal will result in heavy rains in some parts along California's southern coast.
It is possible to lead to precipitation of more than half an inch, which will lead to small waste streams and slippery roads.
It will be the first precipitation for the rainy season.
In late Sunday and early Monday, scattered clouds carrying tropical rain will start approaching Arizona before the rain engulfs a larger area in late Monday and through Tuesday.
The heavy rain will spread over the Four Corners on Tuesday and run until Wednesday.
