More than 20 million people watched Brett Kawanau's hearing
More than 20 million people watched Thursday on six television networks the fascinating spectacle of the testimony of Supreme Court nominee Brett Cavanaugh and Christine Blacey Ford – the woman who accused him of a sexual assault that took place in the 1980s.
During that time, the political standoff continued, with networks interrupting their regular reversal programs in the final minutes of Friday: an agreement that was arranged by Arizonian Senator Jeff Flake and according to which the FBI should conduct a week-long investigation into the allegations.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee that she was 100% convinced that Kavananau was making out with her drunk and tried to take off her clothes during a high school party.
Kavanaugh, in his emotion-deprived testimony, is absolutely adamant that such a thing has never happened.
It's likely that even more of Nielsen's reported number of 20.4 million viewers watched Friday's testimony.
The company has counted average viewers of CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
There was no directly available data on the other networks that broadcast the hearing, including PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network.
Nielsen has also traditionally had difficulty measuring the number of people watching from their workplaces.
By comparison, we're talking about an audience similar in size to that of a championship football match or the Hollywood awards.
Fox News Channel, whose presenters strongly supported Kavanau's appointment, is the first among all networks with an average of 5.69 million viewers during the full-day hearing, Nielsen reported.
ABC was second with 3.26 million viewers.
Nielsen CBS reportedly had 3.1 million, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million, and CNN 2.52 million.
Interest has remained high even after the hearing.
Flake was the central figure in Friday's drama.
After the moderate Republican's office issued a statement that he would vote in favour of Kawanau, CNN and CBS cameras filmed on Friday booing protesters while trying to catch an elevator for a meeting of the Judiciary Committee.
During all the bullying for several minutes, he never lifted his eyes from the ground, which was broadcast live on CNN.
“I’m right here, I’m standing in front of you,” a woman said.
“Do you think he presents the truth to the state?
“You have power while many women are completely helpless,” the attacks continued.
Flake retorted that his office had already come out with a declaration and, prior to the closure of the elevator's doors, announced it would rule further during the hearing by the committee.
Cable and broadcast networks ensured a tight live coverage a few hours later, when the Judicial Committee was due to decide whether to transfer Kavananau's nomination to the attention of the full Senate for a vote.
But Flake said he would do so only if he was sure the FBI would review allegations against the nominee next week, which Democrats insist on in opposition.
Flake was partially persuaded by conversations with his friend, Democratic Party senator Chris Koones.
After a conversation with Koones and a few senators, Fleck made a decision.
His choice was definitely essential, as it was obvious that Republicans wouldn't have enough votes to approve Kawanau without the investigation.
President Trump has ordered the FBI to investigate the charges against Kavanau.
