Police clash with Catalan separatists over anniversary of independence vote
Six people were arrested in Barcelona on Saturday after independence protesters clashed with the gendarmerie when thousands joined opposing demonstrations to mark the first anniversary of Catalonia's polarizing vote.
A group of masked separatists who were not allowed by the gendarmerie began throwing egg-and-drust-painted peacekeepers, thus creating dark and dusty clouds on the streets, which are usually teeming with tourists.
Later in the day, other skirmishes arose with the police, with officers resorting to their batons to contain the situation.
For hours, independence-supporting groups shouted “We don’t forget, we don’t forgive,” while the country’s declared to preserve the integrity they fought back with “Spain forever.”
The local press reported that as a result of the protests, fourteen people received medical attention for minor injuries.
Tensions remain high in an independent region even a year after the October 1 referendum was deemed illegal by Madrid, but celebrated by separatist Catalans.
The vast majority voted to cast their votes for independence, although turnout was low, as most opponents of secession boycotted the referendum.
Catalan authorities said almost 1,000 people were injured last year after police tried to stop holding the vote in the region's voters with a number of violent clashes.
Independency-sought groups spent Friday night on the street to prevent a demonstration in support of the national police.
However, the demonstration took place, but it was forced to take a different route.
Narcissus Termes, 68, an electrician who joined the separatist protest with his wife, says he no longer harbors hope for Catalonia's chances of gaining independence.
“Last year was one of our best moments.
“I’ve seen my parents cry with joy when they could vote, but now it seems we’re at a standstill.”
Despite the marked important victory, even with a small difference, in the regional elections in the last December, political parties supporting the independence of Catalonia are experiencing difficulties this year, as many of their best-known leaders are either in self-imposed exile or have been arrested waiting for a trial because of their role in organizing the referendum and the subsequent declaration of independence.
Hoan Piug, a 42-year-old mechanic who recorded on his phone the protest in support of police, says the conflict was provoked by politicians on both sides.
His comment is that “it’s getting more and more tense.”
Oriol Hunqueras, one of nine arrested Catalan leaders in anticipation of a preliminary trial last year, announced that he would run in next year's European Parliament elections.
“My candidacy for the European elections is the best way to counter the regression of democratic values and the repression we see by the Spanish government.”
