Menga's goal brings Livingston's 1-0 victory over Rangers
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston secured the win
Premier League novice Livingston surprised Rangers and inflicted only Steven Gerrard's second loss in his first 18 games as Ibrox club manager.
Dolly Menga's goal proved decisive, and Gary Holt's team aligned in points with second-placed Hibernian.
Gerrard's team remain winless at home in the Premier League this season, and next Sunday faces driver Harts, from whom he is eight points behind in the standings.
But before that, Rangers hosted Rapid Vienna in the Europa League on Thursday.
Meanwhile, Livingston is already in a six-game unbeaten league streak, and manager Holt has yet to sense the taste of the loss since he replaced Kenny Miller last month.
Livingston missed good opportunities against the idle guests
Holt's team should have led in the scoring long before scoring a goal, and his direct style of play extremely hampered Rangers.
Scott Robinson broke through the defense, but his shot went to the side, after which Alan Lithgow shot past the beam after Craig Halket returned the ball with his head to him.
The hosts left Rangers to play the ball, knowing that they can seriously hamper guests from static situations.
And that's how it came to the winning goal.
Rangers committed an infringement, and Livingston played well, with Declan Gallagher and Robinson combining each other and bringing out Menga, who led the ball and scored from the middle of the penalty area.
So far, Rangers have wielded the ball more, but clashed with an impenetrable home defence, and goalkeeper Liam Kelly rarely had to intervene.
This style of play continued in the second half, although Alfredo Morelos forced Kelly to make a save.
Rangers goalkeeper saved Scott Pittman's foot shot, and Lithlow's header went past the goal after another static position for Livingston.
In the penalty area of Levingston rained centered, which, however, were easily cleared, and the referee twice did not respect the claims of the guests for a penalty – first after a fight between Halket and the reserve Glenn Middleton, and then in case of suspicions of playing with his hand.
“Phenomental” by Livingston – Analysis
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland on “Tony Macaroni Arena”
Phenomenal play and a great result for Livingston.
The team played simply superbly, continuing to exceed expectations and improve their performance.
The style of play and the line-up have barely undergone changes since the team's return to the Premier League, but Holt deserves a lot of praise for the way he has transformed the team since his appointment.
It has real heroes on the field.
Captain Halket was unbelievable, organising the defence in a superb way, while Menga continually kept Connor Goldson and Joe Worrell under strain.
At the same time, Rangers showed nothing inspiring.
Under Gerrard's leadership, they've demonstrated some great things, but in this case they were well below those standards.
They lacked the last pass before the goal, having only once managed to split the defense of the hosts – this loss should awaken Rangers, who are currently in the middle of the standings.
