Hurricane Rosé threatens lightning-fasting Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City (although droughts may benefit)
It's rare for Arizona to be hit by a tropical storm, but there's a good chance that's exactly what's happening early next week, while weakening Hurricane Roza is drifting across the southwest desert, bringing with him the risk of lightning flooding.
The National Meteorology Service has already set up posts against lightning floods on Monday and Tuesday for western Arizona, in southern and eastern Nevada, southeastern California and Utah, including in the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to take a different path over Finisk on Tuesday, and late Monday could bring rain.
The National Meteorological Office in Phoenix tweeted that “since 1950, only ten tropical cyclones have maintained tropical storms within 200 miles of Phoenix!!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane within 40 miles of the Arizona border.”
The latest models from the National Hurricane Center predict 2 to 4 inches of rain with insulated amounts of up to 6 inches at Mogolon Rome in Arizona.
Other areas of the southwest deserts, including the Central Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin, are likely to get 1 to 2 inches of rain, with up to 4 inches of precipitation possible in separate locations.
For those who are not threatened by lightning-fast floods, rose-induced rain may actually be a blessing, as the region is severely affected by drought.
Although the floods are extremely serious worries, some of the rain is likely to be useful, as the south-western part is currently in drought.
According to the U.S. Laundry Surveillance Commission, just over 40 percent of Arizona suffers from at least an excessive drought, which is the second-largest category," weather.com reported.
First, Hurricane Rosé’s route leads to reachland at the Baja California peninsula of Mexico.
Rose, which on Sunday morning still boasts a maximum wind speed of 85 mph, is located 385 miles from Punta Eugenia, Mexico, moving north at 12 miles per hour.
The storm occurs with cooler waters over the Pacific Ocean and therefore weakens.
Therefore, it is expected to react to land in Mexico with the force of a tropical storm on Monday afternoon or in the evening.
Rain over a part of Mexico can be strong and lead to a significant risk of suspenses.
“Total rains of up to 6 inches are expected from Baja California to northwest Sonora, up to 10 inches possible,” weather.com reported.
Rosa will then head north through Mexico as a tropical storm before reaching the Arizona boundary early Tuesday morning as a tropical depression, which will then pass through Arizona in southern Utah by Tuesday late evening.
“The main risk of Rosa or its consequences is extremely heavy rain in Baja California, northwest Sonora, and the desert southwest of the United States,” the National Hurricane Center warned.
These rains are expected to lead to life-threatening lightning floods and waste-carrying streams in the deserts, as well as landslides in mountainous terrains.
