Hundreds die after earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia, death toll rises
At least 384 people lost their lives after many were swept away by swept the coast giant waves when a strong earthquake and tsunami struck the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, authorities said on Saturday.
Hundreds of people had gathered for a beach festival in the city of Palu on Friday, when at nightfall the coast was swept away by waves up to six meters (18 feet high) swept to their deaths and destroyed everything in their path.
The tsunami struck after a 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
“When the tsunami threat rose yesterday, people were still on the beach and didn’t run away immediately, which is why many fell victim to the disaster,” Sutopo Purvo Nugroho, spokesman for Indonesia’s Disaster Overcoming Agency BNPB, said during a briefing in Jakarta.
“Water had dragged cars, trees, whole houses, striking everything in its path,” Nugrojo continued, adding that the tsunami had crossed the open sea at speeds of 800km/h (497 m/h) before hitting the coast.
“Some people climbed the trees and thus managed to save themselves from the water,” he added.
Nearly 16,700 people were evacuated at 24 centers in Palu.
Published by the Disaster Agency photos from the air showed multiple destroyed buildings and shops, torn down and twisted bridges, as well as a water-encircled mosque.
Aftershocks continued rocking the coastal city on Saturday.
A series of earthquakes were felt in an area inhabited by 2.4 million people.
Indonesia's Technology Assessment and Application Agency (BPPT) issued a statement that the energy released by Friday's massive earthquake was about 200 times stronger than the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima in World War II.
The declaration also mentions that the geographical location of the city, which is located at the end of a long and narrow bay, may have increased the size of the tsunami.
Nugrojo described the damage as “huge” and specified that thousands of houses, hospitals, shopping centers and hotels had collapsed.
The bodies of some of the victims were found under the ruins of the collapsed buildings, the spokesman added, then added that 540 people had been harmed and 29 were missing.
Nugroho said casualties and damage could have been higher along the coast 300km (190 miles) north of Palu, an area called Donggala, which is located closer to the quake's epicenter.
He also added that “communications are completely truncated and there is no information from Donggala.”
The Red Cross, for its part, pointed out that more than 300 000 people live there, adding that its teams and volunteers are already headed to the affected areas.
“It’s already a tragedy, but it can be aggravated even more.”
On Saturday, the agency was fiercely criticized for not informing about the striking Palu tsunami, although officials explained that the waves had arrived during an announcement.
Shared on social media amateur clips show a man upstairs on a building screaming at people on the street panic warnings about the approaching tsunami.
After a few minutes, a huge water wall crashes into the shore and takes away both cars and buildings.
Reuters was unable to promptly authenticate the clip.
The quake and tsunami led to a severe power outage that halted communications around Palu and so made it difficult for authorities to coordinate rescue efforts.
The army began sending cargo planes with help from Jakarta and other cities, the responsible authorities said, but evacuees still desperately need food and other essential products.
City's airport was reopened only to rescue efforts and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo will visit evacuation centers in Pali on Sunday.
Those killed in the Indonesian tsunami are now over 800.
The situation is extremely bad.
While World Vision employees from Donggala managed to get safely to Palu, where they took refuge in tarpaulin shelters crucified in the courtyard of their office, they went past a number of scenes of devastation, Mr Dosseba said.
“They told me they had seen countless destroyed houses.”
The situation is extremely bad.
Despite work started on rescue and restorative operations emergency groups, some complain that foreign rescuers who have extensive experience in such situations are prevented from reaching Palu.
Under the Indonesian regulations, the financing, dispatch of personnel and supplies from abroad can only be initiated if the affected place is declared a zone of national disaster.
And that hasn't happened yet.
“The disaster is still at the regional level,” said Aulia Ariani, a spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
“Since the government has announced that this is a national disaster, we can accept international aid, but from there they have not yet declared such status.”
While the second night falls over Palu after Friday's earthquake and tsunami, the friends and families of those still unaccounted continue to hope for a miracle and that their loved ones have somehow survived natural disasters.
On Saturday, a little boy was removed from the sewer.
On Sunday, rescuers freed a woman who had been swamped under the ruins for two days along with her mother's body.
Gendon Subandono, the coach of Indonesia's national paragliding team, has trained two of the missing paragliders for the Asian championship, which ended earlier this month in Indonesia.
Among those trapped at the Roa-Roa hotel were his students, Mr Mandagi added.
“As a senior in the paragliding field, I carry my own emotional burden,” he said.
