U.S. should double AI development funding
At a time when China is becoming increasingly active at the front of artificial intelligence development, the U.S. needs to double its budget for research in this area, according to AI researcher and developer Kai-Fu Loi, who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
Comment was made after various U.S. units of the U.S. government made statements on the issue of AI, although the U.S. as a whole does not have an official AI strategy.
During this time China unveiled its plan as early as last year: the country is aiming to become number 1 in terms of innovation in AI by 2030.
“Redoubling the budget to develop AI will be a good start, given that all other countries are far ahead of the U.S. and are about to make further breakthroughs on AI,” Lee said.
Doubling the funding could also double the chances that the next big AI achievement will come from the U.S., as Lee informed CNBC in an interview last week.
Lee, whose book “The Superpowers of AI: China, the Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is the CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of China’s largest AI companies – Face++.
In the 1980s at Carnegie University, Mellon Lee worked on an AI system that beat America's most successful otelo player, then was CEO at Microsoft Research and president of Google's Chinese branch.
Lee pays tribute to the federal government's previous technology competitions, such as the agency's robotics competition for advanced defense research projects, and asked when it would be the next one that would help recognize the new visionaries.
“U.S. researchers often have to work hard to earn government funding,” Lee said.
“It’s not China that’s taking away academic leaders, it’s the corporations,” he said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have hired university luminaires to work on AI.
Lee also argues that changes in immigration policy could help the U.S. strengthen its AI program.
“I think that doctors of AI sciences should be automatically offered green cards,” he said.
U.S
China's National Foundation for Natural Sciences provides funding to people from academic institutions, similar to the way the National Science Foundation and other state organizations fund researchers in the U.S., but the quality of academic work in China is lower.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense established a Joint Center on Artificial Intelligence, which aims to include industry partners and academia, and the White House announced the formation of a Special Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
Also this month, DARPA reported a $2 billion investment in an initiative called AI Next.
As for the NSF, they currently invest more than $100 million a year in AI development.
Meanwhile, the U.S. Legislature, which wanted to set up a National Security Commission on artificial intelligence, has not noted any activity for months.
The Macedonians voted in a referendum on whether to change the name of their country
The citizens of Macedonia voted in a referendum this Sunday whether the name of the state should be changed to the “Republic of North Macedonia”. This is a move that would resolve a decades-long dispute with Greece, which blocked Macedonia’s membership in the European Union and NATO.
Greece, where an area with the name Macedonia exists, argues that the name of its northern neighbor represents claims against its territorial integrity, which is why it vetoes its inclusion in NATO and the EU.
The two governments reached an agreement in June on the proposed new name, but nationalist-minded opponents say the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's Slavic majority.
President Gjorge Ivanov said that he himself will not vote in the referendum, while at the same time a campaign to boycott him casts a shadow of doubt about whether the referendum will be able to reach the minimum 50 percent required to recognize its validity.
“Do you support NATO and EU membership by adopting an agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the name change, which include Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, claim that the price is worth paying in order to achieve the entry of the state of Macedonia formed after the breakup of Yugoslavia in organizations such as the EU and NATO.
“Today I have come to give my vote for the future of the country, for the young Macedonians, so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, as this means a safer life for all of us,” said Olivera Georgievska, a 79-year-old Skopje citizen.
Although the referendum does not have a binding force, enough members of parliament have ruled that they will comply with the outcome of it to such an extent that the vote already has a decisive burden.
In order to change the name of the state, two-thirds of the parliamentary representatives must vote for this.
The State Election Commission said there had been no reports of irregularities by 1 afternoon.
However, turnout was only 16 percent, compared with 34 percent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters visited the polls.
“I came to vote because of my children, our place is in Europe,” said Gyose Tanevski, a 62-year-old citizen from Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and their son Dushko voted in the referendum in Macedonia to change the country's name, which will open the way to joining NATO and the European Union, in Strumica on September 30, 2018.
Vladimir Kavadarkov, 54, in front of Skopje, prepared a small stage and arranged chairs in front of the tents stretched by the boycoting referendum.
“We support NATO and the EU, but we want to join them with our heads proudly raised, not through the servant loophole,” Kavadarkov said.
“Our country can be poor, but we still have dignity.
If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia and become part of the Eurasian Union.”
Prime Minister Zaev argues that NATO membership will lead to the investments needed for Macedonia, as unemployment in the state is over 20 percent.
“I believe that the vast majority will be with us, as over 80 percent of our citizens prefer the EU and NATO,” Zaev said after casting his own vote.
The statesman also said that the positive result will lead to “affirmation of the future of our people.”
Last Monday, Macedonia’s Institute for Policy Studies published the results of a survey, according to which between 30 and 43 percent of voters will participate in the referendum – which is below the required activity.
Another poll by Macedonia's Thelma television reported that 57% of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 percent said they would vote “Yes.”
In order for the referendum to be successful, the turnout must be 50% plus one vote.
If the referendum is unsuccessful, it will represent the first serious blow against the pro-Western government's policies since it won power in May last year.
Watch: Manchester City's Sergio Aguero goes through the entire Brighton defence and scores
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling overcame Brighton's defence and brought Manchester City's 2-0 victory on Saturday at Etihad Stadium in Manchester, England.
Aguero's play-off for his goal in the 65th minute seemed too easy.
It all started when the Argentine scorer was found with a pass in the middle of the pitch.
He outran three Brighton defenders before sprinting with the ball into the empty space forward.
Aguero then found himself surrounded by four players in green.
He crept past one of the defenders, then left behind the others on the border of Brighton's penalty area.
He then passed left to Sterling.
The English striker with one touch brought the ball back to Aguero, who with a shot with his right foot overcame Brighton goalkeeper Matthew Ryan and found the right corner of his goal.
"Aguero is battling some injuries at his feet," City manager Pep Guardiola told reporters.
“We planned for him to play about 55-60 minutes.
And look what's happened.
“We were lucky to have scored a goal right now.”
But Sterling was the one who provided the initial advance of the “sky-blues” in this English Premier League match.
He scored in the 29th minute.
In the situation for the goal, Aguero received a pass deep into Brighton's half.
He immediately dropped an outgoing pass on the left flank to Leroy Sane.
Sane advanced with the ball, then found Sterling with a pass on the far post.
The striker of the “sky-blue” managed to intercept closely, shortly before the ball went out.
