Labour is no longer broad-minded.
The party is not tolerant of those who speak openly
When Momentum activists in my local party voted to censor me, few were surprised.
After all, I'm just the last Labour member of Parliament, who has been told he's not welcome - just because I speak frankly.
My colleague in parliament, Joanne Ryan, has received a similar attitude, as she resolutely confronted anti-Semitism.
In my case, censorship criticizes me for my disagreement with Jeremy Corbyn.
Regarding the importance of responsible economic policy, national security, Europe, topics that are ironically similar to those with which Jeremy disagreed at the time of the previous leaders.
Labour’s Nottingham East news release said on Friday that “we wish meetings would include everyone and be fruitful.”
For most of my eight years as Labour's local representative in parliament, Friday's assemblies were just that.
Unfortunately, today the tone of most assemblies and promises of a “lighter and more measured” policy have long been forgotten, if they have ever been on the agenda at all.
It has become increasingly apparent that different views are not tolerated in the Labour Party and that any view is judged on the basis of how much the party's leadership appeals.
This started soon after Jeremy became a leader, while my colleagues, for whom I used to work, thought they shared similar political views, began to expect me to make a U-turn and take positions that I would never agree with – be it on national security or the EU's single market.
Whenever I make public speeches – no matter what I’m actually saying – there’s a whopping rant on social media calling for a recall, rejecting moderate politics, statements that I don’t belong to Labour.
Like this doesn't just happen to me.
As a matter of fact, I know that I have even gotten more luck from some of my colleagues, since the comments about me are at least mostly of a political nature.
I can only admire the professionalism and purposefulness of those colleagues who stand up to sexist or racist remarks every day without being frightened by them.
One of the biggest disappointments of this era in politics is how such insults have become the norm.
Last week, Jeremy Corbyn said Labour should embrace a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that now the party is far from open-minded, with any sign of mistrust or change of the rules of choice becoming increasingly narrow-minded.
In the last two years, I've received a lot of advice to sit still, not to talk so much, and that "everything will be fine" then.
But that's not why I went into politics.
Ever since I joined Labour 32 years ago as a pupil provoked by Thatcher's government, which through its neglect has led to a literal collapse of my excellent school, I have been fighting for better public services for those most in need of them – either as a local councillor or as a minister.
I've never hid my political views, including during the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could be confused in any way by my political position and my disagreement with the current leadership.
To all those who supported Friday's proposal, I would like to say that when the country is moving towards Brexit, it will harm households, businesses and the public sector, and I don't understand the desire to squander my time and energy in loyalty to the head of Labour.
But in reality, my message is not to Nottingham Momentum, but to my constituents, whether they are members of Labour or not: It is my pride to serve you, and I promise that no threats of recall or political excommunication will prevent me from acting in the way that I consider best in defending your interests.
Krisley Leslie is a member of parliament for Nottingham East
Air 38-17 Melrose: Invincible Air took the lead in the standings
The two late essays may have changed the final result slightly, but there's no doubt that Ayre deserved to triumph in this incredibly interesting Tennent Premier League match.
The team is already leading the standings and the only of ten teams is unbeaten so far.
The better defense and better use of the opportunities proved decisive in the match, from which coach Peter Murchi and his team can be very satisfied.
"I'm happy because the other teams have tested us quite seriously in past games, and we're still unbeaten," Merchi said.
Robin Christie of Melrose said: "Congratulations to Air, they really took advantage of their situations better than us."
Grant Anderson's essay in the 14th minute, followed by Frazier Klimo's successful extra kick, brought Eyre forward in the score but a yellow card for Scottish international Rory Hughes, released to play in the Warriors match, allowed Melrose to catch up in the score after Jason Bagot realized an essay, followed by a failed extra shot.
Klimo increased the lead for Eyre from a penalty kick shortly before halftime, scoring an essay after a solo raid, and then realizing the extra shot, thus making the 17-5 score for Eyre at the break.
But Melrose started the second half well and Patrick Anderson's essay, followed by a successful extra Bagot kick, reduced the lead to just five points.
A lengthy suspension of the game followed due to a severe injury to Ruaride Noth, who was carried out on a stretcher, and after the resumption of the match Eyre increased their advance after an essay on Stafford McDowall and a successful extra shot by Climo.
After that, Ayre Blair captain MacPherson received a yellow card and Melrose again took advantage of their numerical advantage, exerting strong pressure and realizing an essay through Bruce Colvin, followed by a failed extra shot.
However, the hosts regained the presence of the spirit, and when Struan Hutchinson received a yellow card after knocking Klimo without a ball, from the ensuinced penalty McPherson realized an essay with the help of Eyre's advancing offensive line.
Klimo realized the extra shot, and it wasn't long before he did it again after Kyle Rowe caught the kicked ball from David Armstrong and brought winger Gregor Henry on the flank for the hosts' fifth essay.
The Still Game star appears to be heading for a new career in the restaurant business
Still Game star Ford Kiernan appears to be heading into a more direct and warmer industry after it was discovered that he had been appointed director of a licensed restaurant company.
The 56-year-old actor played Jack Jarvis on the BBC's popular show, of which he is also a screenwriter and one of the two stars alongside his longtime comedy partner Greg Hemphile.
The pair have announced that the upcoming ninth season will be the last for the show, with Kiernan already planning his life after Kreiglang.
According to official registers, he is the director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor refrained from commenting, though a Scottish Sun source hinted that Kiernan had intended to get involved in Glasgow's "thriving restaurant business."
“It’s our sea”: Bolivia, who has no access to the sea, hopes that the court will reopen the way to the Pacific Ocean
Sailors are constantly patrolling the rigging-edged maritime headquarters in La Paz.
There's an ocean blue flag flying over public buildings.
The sea bases from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon are adorned with the motto: “The sea belongs to us by right.
“It’s our duty to take it back.”
Throughout Bolivia, which does not have access to the ocean, the remembrance of the lost outlet handed over to Chile after a bloody 19th-century conflict is still alive – as well as the call to sail across the Pacific again.
In fact, this hope is currently strongest in decades, while Bolivia is awaiting a ruling from the international court on October 1, having already passed five years of consideration.
“The power is in Bolivia, there is cheerfulness and unity everywhere, and the country expects a positive result,” Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla said.
Many Bolivians will be looking at judging the international court on the big screen across the country, hoping the Hague tribunal would support Bolivia’s claim and that after decades of negotiations Chile would be obligated to give Bolivia sovereign access to the ocean.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic local president, who is about to head into a disputed re-election battle next year, has also bet a lot and a lot on the court's judgement on Monday.
“We are very close to taking back our access to the Pacific,” Moralez promised last August.
But some analysts believe that the likelihood that the court will decide in favor of Bolivia is quite small. Moreover, even if it ruled in its favor, it would not change things into reality.
The U.N., located in the Netherlands, does not have the authority to take the territory of Chile and has decreed that it will not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
The fact that the ruling of the international court is only six months after hearing the latest arguments points out that the case “is not complicated”, replied Paz Zarate, a Chilean expert on international law.
And instead of contributing to Bolivia's cause, the last four years may have actually harmed her.
“The problem with access to the ocean has been bloated by Bolivia’s current government,” Zarate said.
He then hinted that Morales's belligerent rhetoric had erased Chile's little remaining goodwill.
Bolivia and Chile at some point will continue the negotiations, but it will be extremely difficult after what happened to have a normal dialogue.
The two countries have not maintained diplomatic missions on each other since 1962.
Previous President Eduardo Rodriguez Weltze, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision was taken unusually quickly.
Bolivia will have “the incredible opportunity to open the page of a new era in his relationship with Chile” on Monday, as well as a chance to “end 139 years of disagreement by achieving benefit for both parties.”
Calzadilla also dismissed the idea that Morales – who continues to be one of Latin America’s most popular presidents – has used the ocean access problem as a “political crutch.”
“Bolivia will never waive its right to go out on the Pacific Ocean,” he added.
“Judgment is an opportunity to see that we have to overcome our past problems.”
North Korea says it won't proceed with nuclear disarmament as long as it can't trust U.S
North Korea's Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho says his country will never proceed to nuclear disarmament first if it is unable to trust Washington.
Ri presented his speech on Saturday to the United Nations General Assembly.
He urged the U.S. to keep promises they made during the meeting between the leaders of opposing countries in Singapore.
His comment comes after U.S
Ri called “empty hope” the idea that continuing with U.S. sanctions and objections to a declaration to end the Korean War would cause the North to fall to its knees.
Washington is reluctant to agree to the declaration, without Pyongyang first making a significant gesture on the path of disarmament.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second meeting.
But there is widespread skepticism that Pyongyang is serious about giving up an arsenal, which they view as the only way to ensure their safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to arrange preparations for a second meeting between Kim and Trump.
The fashion shows in Paris reveal the latest scream on massive hats, which will soon be in the fashion stores next to you
If you want to increase your collection of hats or completely block the sun's rays, you don't need to look anymore.
Designers Valentino and Tom Brown have unveiled a range of incredibly large hats from their upcoming SS19 collection, which stunned the catwalk during Paris Fashion Week.
Quite impractical hats caused a wave on Instagram in the summer, with the designers sending their eye-catching creations to stun the fashion community.
Valentino's bulging proposal was an extravagant beige hat with a feather-like wide periphery, which literally swallowed the heads of the models.
The other too huge accessories included jewelry-decorated watermelons, a magic hat, and even pineapple – but they don’t aim to keep warm on your head.
Tom Braun also unveiled a selection of strange masks – and just in time for Halloween.
Many of the colorful masks had stitched lips and reminded not so much of haute couture as of Hannibal Lecter.
One of the designs resembled diving gear complete with snorkel and glasses, while another looked like a melted cone of ice cream.
If you're into such fashion trends, you're lucky.
Fashion specialists predict that the huge hats will soon be sold in fashion stores near you.
Huge hats come very soon after “La Bomb” – the straw hat with a periphery of two feet, which was seen on everyone’s heads – from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult of the extremely impractical hat, which took over social networks, led to the emergence of another huge design on the catwalk – a straw beach bag, almost as big as the swimsuit-wearing model.
Rafia bag with the color of burnt orange, fringed by raffia and adorned with a handle of white leather, was the eye-catching item in Jacquemus' La Riviera SS19 collection during Paris Fashion Week.
Celebrity stylist Luke Armitid told FEMAIL: “I expect to see big hats and beach bags in fashion stores next summer – once the designer has made such a big impression, it’s going to be difficult to ignore the demand for large-scale accessories.”
John Edward: Linguistic skills are essential for global citizens
Independent schools in Scotland have maintained a reputation for academic excellence, and this continues in 2018 with another superb performance of exam results, which has only been enhanced by individual and collective successes in sporting disciplines, art, music and other social sciences.
With up to 30,000 students across Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Association of Independent Schools (SCIS), are aiming to offer the best level to their pupils and their parents.
Independent schools aim to prepare their students for their further education, their chosen career and their place in a global world.
As an educational sector, which can compile and put into action a personal curriculum, we see that modern languages continue to be a popular and desirable subject in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you’re talking to someone in a language that’s understandable, your words manage to get to them.
But if you speak his own language, you touch his heart.”
This is a powerful reminder that we cannot rely only on the English language, when we want to build relationships and trust with the peoples of other countries.
Through the results of this year's exams, we see that languages are in the first place with the highest grades for independent schools.
A total of 68% of students who have studied foreign languages have achieved the high grade A.
The data, which was collected by 74 members of SCIS schools, showed that 72% of pupils achieved an A score in Chinese, with the same score having 72% of those who studied German, 69% of French teachers and 63% of those with Spanish.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland perceive foreign languages as vital skills that children and young people will definitely need in the future.
Currently, languages as a subject of free choice on the part of students receive the same attitude as natural-mathematical disciplines (scientific, technological, engineering and mathematical disciplines) both in the programs of independent schools and beyond.
Conducted in 2014 by the UK's Committee on Employment and Employment Skills showed that from the reasons why employers find it difficult to find employees, 17% have indicated insufficient language competences.
Because of this, more and more foreign language proficiency is becoming mandatory in order to prepare young people for their future careers.
More and more jobs require proficiency in a foreign language, which makes these skills essential in a globalized world.
Regardless of the chosen career, if a person masters a foreign language, he will have a real advantage in the future, after mastering a similar skill that remains for life.
The ability to communicate directly with people from other countries automatically puts the multi-language person one breast ahead of the competition.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adult UK nationals in 2013, 75% were not fluent in a foreign language well enough that they could conduct a language, with French being the only language whose proficiency reached a double-digit rate with an indicator of 15%.
That's why investing in language learning today is important for children now.
Proficiency in several languages, especially those of developing economies, will provide children with a better chance of finding a good job.
In Scotland, each school will differ in terms of taught languages.
A number of schools will focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are considered more important to the UK in the future by 2020, for example Chinese or Japanese.
Whatever interests your child has, there will always be enough languages to choose from in independent schools, and educators will be specialists in their field.
Independent schools in Scotland are determined to provide an educational environment that will prepare children and give them the necessary skills to succeed in life, regardless of what the future holds.
At the moment, it cannot be denied that in a global business environment, languages continue to play an incredibly important role in the future of any country, which necessitates this to be taken into account in the education system.
And indeed, modern languages should actually be considered “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, together with diversity and excellent quality, to young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is director of the Scottish Association of Independent Schools
LeBron will make his Lakers debut this Sunday in San Diego
The wait is almost over for fans eager to see LeBron James' first game with the Los Angeles Lakers team.
Lakers coach Luke Walton has announced that James will take part in the team's first pre-season game against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego.
But it is not yet specified how many minutes he will play in the match.
“He’s going to be on the field for between one and 48 minutes,” Walton jokingly announced via the Lakers’ official website.
Lakers reporter Mike Trudell announced at Tweeter that it was likely James would play relatively few minutes in the match.
After training earlier in the week, James was asked about his plans regarding the Lakers' six pre-season games.
“At this stage of my career, I don’t need pre-season matches to prepare for the new season,” he said.
Trump rally in West Virginia, YouTube channel
President Donald Trump begins a series of campaign rallies tonight in Whilling, West Virginia.
It's the first of five planned Trump rallies that include stops in familiar places, including Tennessee and Mississippi.
By postponing the confirmatory vote for his choice to fill the vacancy in the Supreme Court, Trump aims to support the upcoming mid-term elections because Republicans are threatened with a loss of congressional control when casting votes in November.
What time will the Trump rally take place in West Virginia tonight and how can you watch online?
Trump's visit to Willing, West Virginia is scheduled for 19 p.p.m., eastern time tonight, Saturday, September 29, 2018.
You can watch Trump's visit to Weiling, West Virginia, below via the live broadcast on YouTube.
Trump is likely to consider this week's hearings for Supreme Court nominee Brett Cavanaugh, in which tensions emerged over allegations of immoral sexual acts, which detained the Senate's confirmatory vote to a week while an FBI investigation was ongoing.
However, the main goal of this series of rallies is to help Republicans gain momentum for the November election.
That’s why in President Donald Trump’s campaign, it was announced that those five rallies next week aimed at “activating volunteers and supporters as Republicans try to protect and expand their majority in the Senate and in the House of Representatives,” according to Reuters.
"Controllage control is so essential to its agenda that the president will visit as many states as possible, and we're starting a campaign season full of engagements," a Trump campaign spokesman who declined to mention it told Reuters.
Tonight's rally planned for Wesbanco Arena in Weiling could lead supporters from "Ohio and Pennsylvania and attract media attention from Pittsburgh," according to West Virginia Metro News.
On Saturday, Trump's second visit will take place for this month in West Virginia, the state in which he won by more than 40 percent in 2016.
Trump is trying to help West Virginia's Senate candidate, Republican Patrick Morrissey, who is lagging behind in the polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Maurice the fact that the president has come to give him a boost in the polls,” Simon Heather, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia, told Reuters.
Ryder Cup 2018: The U.S. team will fight to the end, keeping their hopes alive ahead of Sunday's singles
After three consecutive sessions without intrigue “foursom” matches on Sunday afternoon maybe brought what “Rider Cup” needed.
Changing the advantage in the sport is a rather subjective concept, in which, however, players believe unreservedly, even more so in competitions like this.
So, which team is in a better position right now?
“They led by six points, and now they’re only four, so I guess we’ve gained some momentum,” Jordan Speath said at the end of today’s racing day.
Of course, Europe has an advantage of four points with only twelve more for distribution.
As Speath puts it, however, Americans have more to show and their fans shouldn't lose hope if we have to judge by the sporting form of Speet and Justin Thomas, who played together throughout the day and each of them took three out of four possible points.
Speath shows excellent form from the beginning to the end of each hole and is the true leader of his team.
The encouraging cheers of the fans were getting louder as he progressed in the round in which, with a precise shot, he leveled the scores in the fourth match, after, together with Thomas, trailed by two strokes after the first two holes.
His shot, which won them the match on the 15th hole, was met with strong rapturous reactions that make us believe that really all is not lost for the U.S. team.
“It’s important that you don’t think about anything else and focus on your own match,” Speath said.
That's all that's left for those players now.
18 holes to be remembered.
The only players with more points than Speath and Thomas over the past two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Flitwood, the undisputed heroes of “Rider Cup” so far.
The bizarrely but admirable couple from Europe won four out of four and it seems everything works out for them.
“Mollywood” was the only couple who didn’t “god” on Sunday afternoon, but they also didn’t have “goddies” either on Saturday morning or Friday afternoon or morning.
These consistent successes, as well as the way it seemed to pass energy between them and the enthusiastic fans, have cemented the view that they are the players who will win on Sunday and there will be no better pair than Flitwood and Molinari, which will bring Europe’s potential victory as the sun sets over the Le Golf National.
Preferably both at the same time on different holes.
Tales of European fame, however, turned out to be somewhat premature.
Buba Watson and Webb Simpson quickly dealt with Sergio Garcia, the hero of “fourball” matches of the morning, while he partnered with Alex Noren.
“Bogi” and two “doubles” on the first nine holes put the Spaniard and the Swede into problems that they failed to deal with.
However, there is no way in Sunday's games anyone else can solve your problems.
“Fourball” and “Foursom” matches are extremely interesting to watch closely because of the interactions between the pairs, the advice they exchange with each other, and the way in which a strategy can undergo changes at any moment.
The Europeans played a better team and that’s why they gained a significant lead before the last day, but this session of “foursoam” matches showed that the American team is also ready to fight to the last – something that a lot of people, especially in the United States, strongly doubted.
Europe leads by 10–6 on the last day of the Ryder Cup.
Europe will have a solid lead before the last day of the Ryder Cup, after leading the U.S. 10–6 after playing matches from the formats “Fourball” and “Foursoam” on Saturday.
The inspiring duo Tommy Flitwood and Francesco Molinari started with two wins against the unconvincing Tiger Woods, with which their asset of the race at “Le Golf National” rose to four points.
In an attempt to regain the trophie he lost to “Hazeltine” two years ago, Thomas Bjorn’s European team dominated the morning “fourball” matches over Americans who made a mistake after a mistake, and in the end Europe won the series with 3–1 wins.
The U.S. put up more resistance in “foursom” matches, where they won two wins, but failed to reduce the gap.
Jim Furick's team needs 8 points from Sunday's 12 singles games to keep the trophie.
Flitwood is the first rookie from Europe to earn four points in a row, and he and Molinari, known as “Mollywood”, after this sensational weekend became only the second pair to have won four points from their first four games in Ryder Cup history.
After unequivocally outplaying Woods and Patrick Reid in “fourball” matches, they were extremely motivated to defeat the broken Woods and American rookie Bryson Decambu with an even more emphatic score 5&4.
Woods, who barely went through his two matches on Saturday, only occasionally demonstrated outbursts of his genius, but so far there have been 19 losses from 29 games in “fourball” and “foursom” matches, as well as a total of seven consecutive losses.
Justin Rose, given a break for morning “fourball” matches, has partnered Henrik Stansson in “foursom” clashes in 2&1 victory over Dustin Johnson and Brooks Kopka – number one and number three in the world respectively.
However, the team of Europe failed to enjoy an entirely pleasant, cool and cloudless day southwest of Paris.
Three-time winner Jordan Speath and Justin Thomas returned the Americans' hopes by winning two points on Saturday.
They achieved a difficult 2§1 victory against Spaniard John Rahm and Jan Polter in “fourball” matches, and later returned to beat Polter and Rory McIlroy with 4&3 in the “foursoam” matches, although they lost the first two holes.
Only twice in the history of the “Ryder Cup” it has happened that a team catches up four points deficit before the singles, and according to the regulation, Furik's team will keep the trophy even in a draw.
However, having been outplayed in two straight days, it seems their chances of a turnaround on Sunday aren't particularly great.
North Korea says there is “no way” to disarm unilaterally, without trust
North Korea's foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that ongoing sanctions are deepening distrust of the U.S. and there is no way for the country to abandon its nuclear weapons unilaterally under such circumstances.
At the annual general meeting of the world organization, Ri Young-hoe said “significant goodwill measures” have been taken in the last year, for example, stopping nuclear and missile tests, destroying the nuclear test site and promises not to spread nuclear weapons and technology.
“However, we don’t see a corresponding reaction from the U.S.,” he said.
“Without confidence in the U.S., we won’t believe in our own national security, and under no circumstances is there a way to disarm unilaterally first.”
While Ri was expressing North Korea’s familiar complaints about Washington’s resistance to a “phased” approach to removing nuclear weapons under which North Korea would be rewarded because it had taken step-by-step measures, his statement seemed significant because it did not reject unilateral nuclear disarmament, as Pyongyang did in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement from Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first summit between the U.S. president and the North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim pledged to work for “nuclear disarmament of the Korean Peninsula,” and Trump gave North Korea security guarantees.
North Korea is aiming for an official end to the 1950–1953 Korean War, but the U.S. says Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington also opposed calls to ease tough international sanctions against North Korea.
“The U.S. is demanding that “we first remove nuclear weapons” and increases pressure through sanctions to achieve its goal through coercion, and even objects to the ‘declaration for the end of the war,’” Ri said.
“Believing that sanctions can make us fall to our knees is a meaningless dream of those who don’t know us.
However, the problem is that the ongoing sanctions exacerbate our mistrust."
Ri didn't mention whether it was planned to hold a second meeting between Kim and Trump, for which the U.S. leader stressed through the U.N. earlier this week.
Instead, the minister emphasized on the three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months and added: “If the country on the issue of nuclear disarmament was South Korea, not the United States, the abolition of nuclear weapons on the Korean peninsula would not have come to such a standstill.
However, the tone of Ri’s speech was significantly different from the previous year, when he told the U.N. General Assembly that directing North Korean missiles to the U.S. mainland was inevitable after “evil President” Trump called Kim a “man-missile” on a suicide mission.
This year at the United Nations, Trump, who last year threatened North Korea with “complete destruction”, praised Kim for his courage in taking disarmament measures, but said there was still a lot of work to be done and sanctions should remain in place until North Korea removes nuclear weapons.
On Wednesday, Trump stressed that there is no deadline for this and said: “It doesn’t matter if it takes two years, three years or five months.”
China and Russia say the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday that: “The implementation of Security Council sanctions must necessarily continue until full, final and confirmed nuclear disarmament takes place.”
The Security Council has unanimously stepped up sanctions against North Korea since 2006 in an effort to stop funding Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met Ri on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly and then said he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare a second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip went well.
He left Pyongyang in July, saying progress had been made, but after just a few hours, North Korea had accused him of making "ganger demands."
At a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea vowed to destroy a missile site and a nuclear complex if the U.S. took “relevant measures.”
According to him, Kim told him that the “relevant measures” he aspires to are the security guarantees that Trump promised in Singapore and that he wishes to achieve a normalization of relations with Washington.
Students at Harvard on course for enough rest
A new course at Harvard University this year prompts all students to sleep more in an effort to combat a growing “all-night” learning culture with the help of caffeine.
A lecturer found that students at the world's number one university often don't even think twice when it comes to the very basics of how to take care of themselves.
Charles Keysler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard School of Medicine and a specialist at Brigham Hospital and the Women's Hospital, drafted the course he thought was the first of its kind in the U.S.
He was inspired to start the course after talking about the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
Finally, a girl came to me and said, “Why are they telling me only now, in my last year?”
"She said no one had ever spoken to her about the importance of the dream, which surprised me," he told The Telegraph.
The course, which is held for the first time this year, explains to students the most essential of how good sleep habits help academic and athletic performance, as well as to improve general well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health departments, said the university had decided to introduce the course after it was established that students were suffering from a serious lack of sleep during the week.
The one-hour course includes a series of interactive tasks.
In one part, there's an image of a dormitory room where students click cups of coffee, curtains, sneakers, and books to explain to them the effects of caffeine and light, how athletic performance is affected by lack of sleep, and the importance of bedtime routine.
In another part of the participants it is told how long-term deprivation of sleep can increase the risks of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
A campus map with interactive icons then encourages participants to think about their day-to-day routine.
“We don’t know it won’t immediately change the behavior of students.
But we believe they have the right to know – just as you have the right to know the health effects of choosing to smoke,” Keysler added.
Pride culture of “learning all night” still exists, he says, adding that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure on students mean sleep deprivation is a growing problem.
Ensuring enough quality sleep should be the “secret weapon” of students to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he says – even in order to avoid weight gain, as sleep deprivation pushes the brain into starvation mode, causing them to be constantly hungry.
Raymond Seo, a 19-year-old student from California who studied chemical and physical biology, assisted Professor Kaysler in drafting the course by taking one of his classes during his first year at Harvard.
He says his course “opened his eyes” and inspired him to spread it across the campus.
It is hoped that the next step is for all graduate students to be asked to complete a similar curriculum before joining the competitive institution.
Professor Keysler recommends that students think about whether to set a bed-and-awaken alarm and know the harmful effects of “blue light” emanating from electronic screens and LED lighting, which can upset the circadian rhythm and lead to trouble falling asleep.
Livingston 1 – 0 Rangers: Menga goal crushed Gerrard's team
Rangers suffered yet another loss after Dolly Menga's jester crushed Steven Gerrard's idle team and brought a 1–0 victory for Livingston.
The Ibrox team wanted to record their first win as a guest from a 4–1 triumph over St. Johnston, but Gary Holt's team inflicted only Gerrard's second defeat in his 18 games as a manager, which left his team eight points short from the detached Harts Premier League leader of Ladbrokes.
Menga scored seven minutes before the break, and Rangers' deprived structural ideas team at any point didn't seem close to equalising.
After the game Rangers slumped in sixth place and Livingston are now third, trailing the second Hibernian only on goal difference.
For Rangers, there could also be further problems, as side referee Callum Spence suffered a head wound after being hit with an object that was apparently thrown by guest fans.
Gerrard made eight changes to the team that defeated Ayre in the semi-finals of the Scottish League Cup.
Holt, on the other hand, came up with the same Eledeon of Livingston, who snatched a point against Harts last week, and should be happy with the way his aggressive team parried all the opponent's actions.
Rangers may have dominated possession of the ball, but Livingston did better on rarer occasions when they owned it.
They should have scored further in the second minute when Menga led Scott Pittman with one touch against goalkeeper Alan McGregor, but the midfielder missed the good opportunity.
Later centering Keegan Jacobs from the foul found captain Craig Halket, but his defence partner Alan Litgow deflected the ball to the side from the back beam.
Rangers took control of the game but failed to create anything meaningful in the final third of the pitch.
Alfredo Morelos definitely thought there should be a penalty in the 15th minute when he collided with Stephen Lawles, but referee Stephen Thomson dismissed the Colombian's claims.
Rangers delivered only two accurate shots in the first half, but former Ibrox goalkeeper Liam Kelley handled Lassana Koulibaly's head-up shot easily and Ovie Edjaria's weak shot.
Although Livy's goal in the 34th minute was surprising in terms of possession of the ball, it was fully deserved.
Rangers once again failed to cope with a deep Jacobs centre-back.
Scott Arfield were unresponsive when Declan Gallagher sent the ball to Scott Robinson, who coolly handed to Menga, for whom it was easy to realize.
