Livingston, however, kept forcing guests to play in a home-friendly style of play, with Lithgow and Halket continually weaning the long balls sent forward.
Holt's team could have increased their lead in the final minutes, but McGregor was able to cope with Jacobs' shot, and at the ensuing corner Lithgow fired a header past the beam.
Glenn Middleton from Rangers demanded a penalty at the end of the match after a single fight with Jacobs, but Thomson again saw nothing wrong with the situation.
Yearbook: Inventor of the geiger counter
Page from our yearbook Sunday Morning: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago and COUNTED... the day future physicist Johann Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the creation of the device, known as Geiger counter.
A milestone in science since then, the Geiger counter has also become a cornerstone of pop culture, when the film The Coronado Bells came out in 1950, which starred seemingly incredible scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans.
Man: “What on earth is this
Rogers: It's a Geiger counter and it's used to detect radioactive minerals, for example, uranium..
“When you put on these headphones, you can actually hear the effects of the atoms released by radioactivity in minerals.”
Evans: “It certainly pops right now!”
“Hans” Geiger died in 1945, just a few days after his 63rd birthday.
But the discovery, which bears his name, continues to live on.
New cancer vaccine could train the immune system to 'see' foreign cells
A new cancer vaccine can train the immune system to “see” foreign cells and kill them
The vaccine trains the immune system to recognize foreign cells as part of the treatment
The method involves extracting immune cells from the patient and modifying them in a laboratory
They can then “see” a protein that is common to many types of cancer and are reinjected into the patient
The trial vaccine showed promising results in patients with different cancers.
A woman treated with the vaccine, which trains the immune system to recognize foreign cells, saw her ovarian cancer disappear for more than 18 months.
The method involves extracting immune cells from the patient and modifying them in a laboratory so that they can “see” a protein common to many types of cancer called HER2, and then re-injected into the patient.
According to Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland: “Our results suggest that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 “stimulates the growth of several types of cancer,” including breast, ovaries, lungs and colorectal carcinoma, explained Prof. Berzofski.
A similar approach of taking immune cells from patients and “educating” them how to target cancer cells was used in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
