"Game of Thrones" star Kit Harrington is against toxic masculinity
Kit Harrington is known for his role as Jon Snow, avoluminously-savvy HBO fantasy series The Game of Thrones.
But the 31-year-old actor spoke out against the stereotype of the macho character, saying that showing such roles on the screen leads youngsters often to think that they need to be “raw” to be respected.
In an interview with The Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he believed “something went wrong” and asked how to deal with the problem of toxic masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently married his Game of Throne partner Rose Leslie, also 31, admits he feels “firmly convinced” that this problem needs to be solved.
“At the moment, I’m firmly convinced – what have we messed up about masculinity?” he said.
“What are we teaching adolescent men about the problem we’re seeing now?”
Kit believes television may be partly responsible for increasing toxic masculinity due to many masculine characters.
He continued, “What’s innate and what’s being taught?
What is taught by television and on the street that makes boys feel they have to become this particular type of men?
I think this is really one of the big questions of our time – how do we change it?
“Something is confusing for young men.”
In the interview, he also admitted that he would not participate in a prequel or sequels to Game of Thrones after the series ended next summer, claiming that he had "ended the battlefields and horses."
