What is the “Global Citizen” festival and does it help to reduce poverty at all?
This Saturday, New York City will host the “Global Citizen” festival, an annual musical event that has an impressive lineup of performers and performs an equally impressive mission: eradicating poverty around the world.
Now, in its seventh year, the “Global Citizen” festival will bring together tens of thousands of people at the grand lawn in Central Park, who will not only enjoy the performances of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but will also get acquainted with the true purpose of the event – eliminating extreme poverty by 2030.
The “Global Citizen” festival, which was launched in 2012, is a follow-up to the “Global Poverty” project, an international protection group that hopes to eliminate poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting it.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (unless you want to pay for a VIP ticket), visitors had to complete a number of tasks or “actions” such as voluntary participation, sending not an email to a world leader, calling by phone or otherwise in order to help raise awareness of their goal of eradicating poverty.
But how successful is “Global Citizen” for 12 years before achieving its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free giggling an ingenious way to be persuaded to demand action or is it just another case of so-called “clictivism” – do people think they’re doing something significant when signing an online petition or sending a post on Twitter?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has recorded more than 19 million “actions” from his supporters pushing for many different purposes.
It says these actions have helped world leaders announce commitments and policies equivalent to more than $37 billion, which will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 engagements and announcements arising from its actions, and at least $10 billion has already been repaid or collected from them.
The group estimates that the funds provided have already directly impacted nearly 649 million people worldwide.
Some of the main commitments include “The Power of Nutrition,” a UK-established partnership between investors and contractors, committing to “helping children develop their full potential,” promising to provide Rwanda with $35 million to help end malnutrition in the country after more than 4700 Twitter posts were received from “global nationals.”
“With the support of the UK government, donors, national governments and global citizens like you, we can turn the social injustice of malnutrition simply into a historical memo,” Ambassador Tracey Ullman told the audience at a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that once more than 5,000 actions have been taken calling on Britain to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government has announced funding for a project, the "Power of Nutrition", which will reach 5 million women and children.
In response to one of the FAQs on his website asking “Why do you think we can end extreme poverty?”
“Global citizen” replied: “This is going to be a long and difficult path – sometimes we will fail and fail again.”
But just as with the big civil rights and anti-apartheid movement before us, we will succeed, because we are stronger together.
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi Bi, Janelle Mona are just some of the performers at this year's New York event, at which Deborah-Lee Ferness and Hugh Jackman will headline.
