U.S. ambassador accuses China of 'harasses' with 'propaganda ads'
One week after an official newspaper in China released a four-page advertisement in a U.S. daily newspaper about the mutual benefits of U.S.-China trade, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
Last Wednesday, U.S. President Donald Trump cited China Daily's paid publication in the Des Moines Register – the best-selling newspaper in Iowa – after accusing China of seeking to interfere in the U.S
Trump's indictment that Pecky was trying to intervene elections for the U.S. Congress noted what U.S. officials told Reuters was a new phase in Washington's escalating campaign to put pressure on China.
While it's normal for foreign governments to run ads to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently engaged in an escalating trade wreak in which they are leveling tariffs on imports between countries.
According to U.S. and Chinese experts at the start of the trade war, tariffs in China were designed to hit exporters in states such as Iowa, who support Trump's Republican Party.
Terry Branstadt, the U.S. ambassador to China and a former longtime governor of Iowa, a major exporter of agricultural goods to China, said Beijing has harmed U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
China, Branstad wrote in a commentary on Sunday’s Des Moines Register, “currently doubling this bullying by releasing propaganda ads into our own free press.”
“In spreading its propaganda, China’s government has taken advantage of America’s respected tradition of free speech and the press by rolling out a paid advertisement in the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
“In contrast, on the newspaper stand down the street here in Beijing, you’ll find limited speeches, and you won’t see any real coverage of the divergent opinions that the Chinese people may have regarding China’s disturbing economic trajectory, given that the media is under the strict control of the Chinese Communist Party,” he wrote.
