“Women won’t forget what happened yesterday – they won’t forget it either tomorrow or in November,” Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser and national spokesman for the progressive group MoveOn, said on Friday, according to the Washington newspaper.
On Friday morning morning, protesters chanted “November is coming!” during a demonstration in the Senate corridor as Republicans who overset the Judiciary Committee decided to proceed with Kavano’s nominee despite Dr. Christine Blasius Ford’s testimonials, Mic reported.
"Democratic enthusiasm and motivation will be excluded," Stu Rothenberg, a non-partite political analyst, told the news.
“People say that the problem is already serious; it’s true.
But it could become even more surerious, especially among female voters in the suburbs and younger voters, between the ages of 18 and 29, who often don’t vote because they don’t like the president.”
Even before Ford's public testimony detailing her allegations of sexual assault on the Supreme Court nominee, analysts suggest a backlash could follow if Republicans push to confirm the candidacy.
“It’s become a big mess for the Republican Party,” said Michael Steele, former chairman of the Republican National Committee early last week, according to NBC News.
“It’s not just about the committee’s vote, the final ballot, or whether Kavanaugh was put on the bench. It’s about the way Republicans deal with it and how they treat it,” Guy Cecil, director of Priorities USA, a group that is helping to elect Democrats, told the news channel.
Americans, however, seem somewhat divided on the question of whom to believe in the testimony of Ford and Kavanaugh, with a little more leaning towards the second.
A new poll from YouGov shows that 41 percent of respondents definitely or likely believe Ford's testimony, and 35 percent say they definitely or likely trust Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38 percent said Kavanaugh probably or certainly lied during his testimony, while 30 percent claimed the same for Ford.
After Flake's insistence, the FBI is currently investigating Ford's claims, as well as at least one other accuser, Debora Ramirez, The Guardian reported.
Last week, Ford testified under oath before the Senate Judiciary that Kavanaugh assaulted her while intoxicated when she was 17.
Ramirez claimed that the Supreme Court candidate showed his genitals in front of her while they were at a party during her studies at Yale in the 1980s.
The World Wide Web inventor plans to launch a new internet to attack Google and Facebook
World Wide Web inventor Tim Berners-Lee is launching a venture that aims to rival Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest project of technology legend, Inrupt, is a company that builds Berners-Lee Solid's open-source platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people have permission to access certain information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind Inrupt was a “world dominance.”
“We have to do it now,” he said regarding the venture.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid technology, which allows people to create their own “online repository of personal data” or POD.
It contains lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library, and other personal and professional instruments.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack, and Spotify, which are available through any browser, and at the same time it's all of them at the same time.
What's unique in the online repository of personal data is that the user determines who has access to what information.
The company calls it “giving powers of personal data.”
The idea for Inrupt, according to the company's CEO John Bruce, is for the company to invest resources, processes, and appropriate skills to help Solid become accessible to everyone.
Currently, the company consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, an IBM-bought security platform, several developers who have contracted to work on the project, and a group of volunteer programmers.
From this week, technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team didn't talk to Facebook and Google about whether to make a complete change or not, when all of their business models are completely updated overnight.
“We don’t ask their permission.”
In a post on Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee tweeted that Inrupt's mission was to provide energy for trading and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new Solid-based network.
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he created the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Technical Institute.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has had a strong influence in the network's neutrality debate.
Despite launching Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I’m extremely optimistic about this new era on the internet,” Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Van: in memory of the priest who was awarded the Victoria Cross during the First World War
It is celebrated for 100 years in the hometown of the only Anglican priest, who was awarded the cross Victoria for his participation in fighting during the First World War.
Lieutenant Colonel Reverent Bernard Van won the award on September 29, 1918 in the attack of Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
Four days later, however, he was killed by a sniper, not realizing that he had been honored with Britain's highest military honors.
A commemorative monument was unveiled on Saturday by his two grandchildren at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of his grandchildren, Michael Wang, said it was “extremely symbolic” for the monument to be unveiled for exactly 100 years of his grandfather’s work, awarded a medal.
According to London Gazette, on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Van led his battalion through Canal de Saint-Quentin “in very thick fog and under heavy fire from artillery and machine guns.”
Later, he attacked the firing line with “incredible bravery” and led forward before attacking an artillery cannon with one hand and knocking out three of the squad.
Lieutenant Colonel Wang was assassinated by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month before the end of the war.
Michael Wang, 72, said his grandfather’s actions were “something I know I could never do, but it makes me humble.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vang, placed a wreath after the parade, led by “Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.”
Michael Vang said it was “a great honor to participate in the parade” and added “the valor of the true hero is demonstrated through the support that will be provided to many people.”
MMA fans waited all night to watch the Bellator 206, but instead they got Peppa Pig
Imagine you've been waiting all night to watch a full Bellator 206 edition, and in the end you can't witness the main event.
The San José programme contained 13 fights, including six of the main card and was supposed to be broadcast live at night in Britain on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m., just as Gegard Moussasi and Rory Macdonald were preparing to face each other, viewers in Britain remained stunned when the show switched to Peppa Pig.
Some were disappointed after they were awake until the early hours, especially because of the fight.
Fenn described on Twitter switching the children’s cartoon as “some kind of sick joke.”
“The government regulates that at 6 a.m. this content is not appropriate, so the program was replaced with a children’s transmission,” said Dave Schwartz, Bellator’s senior vice president of marketing and communications, in response to a question about broadcasting.
“Peppa Pig, yes.”
Bellator President Scott Cocker has said they will be working on scheduling the meetings to include viewers from Britain in the future.
“In my opinion, when it comes to repetitions, we can solve the problem,” Cocker said.
“But it’s six in the morning on Sunday and we can’t plan things so that here the event will take place on Sunday, and there – on Monday.
After all, we're working on it.
Believe me, when the program was changed, we received a lot of messages and not everyone was friendly.
We tried to fix it as we thought it was a technical problem.
But it wasn't like that, it was government.
I can promise you that's not going to happen next time.
We’re going to stick to incorporating five fights instead of six – as we usually do. We’ve tried to provide more to the fans, but we’ve gone overboard.
It’s an unpleasant situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daly felt 'lower' due to his sexuality
Olympic water jumper Tom Dalley says he grew up feeling inferior to everyone else due to his sexuality – but that motivated him to succeed.
The 24-year-old athlete claimed that while he didn’t start studying in middle school, he didn’t realize that “not everyone is like me.”
In an interview with the first edition of Desert Island Discs on Radio 4 led by Lauren Laverne, he spoke about gay rights to give “hope” to others.
He also said that when he became a parent, he became less interested in winning the Olympics.
Permanent presenter of the long-running show, Kirsty Young, has been in hospital for a few months.
Starring in Laverne Daley’s first program said he felt “less” than everyone else while he was growing up because “it wasn’t socially acceptable to like both girls and boys.”
He said, “Until today, the feeling of inferiority and being different from others are actually the things that give me the energy and power to succeed.”
He wanted to prove that it was “something,” he said so as not to disappoint anyone when he eventually learned of his sexuality.
The two-time Olympic bronze medalist has become an active participant in LGBT campaigns and is using his participation in this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to call on more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he was doing this because he was happy that he could live openly, without consequences, and he wanted to inspire “hope” to others.
The three-time world champion said that when he fell in love with a man — American director Dustine Lance Black, whom he met in 2013 — had “surprised him.”
Daley married the Oscar-winner, who was 20 years older than him last year, but claimed the age gap was never a problem.
“When you go through so much while you’re so young,” he participated for the first time in the Olympics at the age of 14, and his father died of cancer three years later, he said, “it’s hard to find someone your age who’s experienced similar ups and downs.”
The couple became parents in June and have a son named Robert Ray Black-Daily, and Daley himself says "his whole worldview" has changed.
“If you had asked me last year, I would have told you that I was only interested in winning a gold medal,” he said.
“You know, there are more important things than Olympic gold medals.
My Olympic gold medal is Robbie.”
His son is named after his father, Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father couldn’t accept that he was going to die, and one of the last things he asked was if they already had tickets to the 2012 London Olympics – since he wanted to be in the front row.
“I couldn’t tell him ‘you won’t be there for me, Dad, not in the front row,’” he said.
“I held his hand until he stopped breathing and at that moment when I found out he was dead, I finally admitted he wasn’t invulnerable,” he said.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympiad and won a bronze medal.
“I just knew I had dreamed about it all my life – jumping into the Olympics in front of the audience at home. There wasn’t a better feeling,” he said.
This also inspired the choice of his first song – “Proud” by Heather Small – which accompanied him during his preparations for the Olympics and still makes him goosebumps.
Desert Island Discs airs on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday from 11:15 a.m., British summer weather.
Lost form Mikkelsson remains on the bench for Ryder Cup on Saturday
American Phil Mikkelson will set a record on Sunday, when he will play his 47th match at the Ryder Cup, but he will have to regain his old form if he doesn't want his record to be about failure.
Mikelsson, who participated in the two-year-old event for the record 12th time, was left on the bench by captain Jim Furik for “fourball” and “foursam” matches on Saturday.
Instead of again being a major figure in the U.S. team, the five-time winner of major tournaments spent his day as a fan and in training his shots from a distance in hopes of correcting his weaknesses.
The 48-year-old athlete, who even at the top of his career was never among the most straight-forward players, is not in optimal shape for the difficult Le Golf National court, where a long rough pitch usually punishes every wrong shot.
And if the course alone isn't disturbing enough, in Sunday's ninth game on Sunday Mikkelson will go up against precise British Open champion Francesco Molinari, who is in a team with rookie Tommy Flitwood and looking to win all their four games this week.
If the Americans, who are trailing by four points ahead of the 12 singles games, make a strong start, it may be that Mikelson's match is absolutely crucial.
Furik expressed confidence in his team-mate, although he could hardly say anything very differently.
“He fully understood his role today, tapped my back, hugged me and said he would be ready tomorrow,” Furick said.
“He is confident in himself.
He is a member of the Hall of Fame and has given so much both in the past and this week.
I probably didn't imagine him playing in just two games.
I expected that he would play more, but that's just how it turned out and we believe that we made the right decision.
He wants to be on the golf course and play, just like everyone else.”
Mikkelson will break Nick Faldo's record for the most games in the Ryder Cup played on Sunday.
This could put an end to his Ryder Cup career, which has never come close to his impressive individual achievements.
Mikkelson has 18 wins, 20 losses and seven equal games, but Furik said his presence also brings other benefits to the team, which are not measured by figures.
“He’s funny, sarcastic, witty, he likes to joke with people and he’s a great guy for any team,” he explained.
“I think the younger players had fun with him, especially this week.
“He’s doing a lot more than just playing.”
Thomas Bjorn, Europe's captain, is aware that the big lead could soon be melted away
The captain of the Europe team, Thomas Bjorn, knows from personal experience that the big lead, accumulated before the singles matches from the last day of the “Ryder Cup”, can be easily erased.
The Dane made his debut in Valderrama’s 1997 match, when the team with captain Seve Ballisteros led by five points ahead of the Americans, but it was right on the final straight that it was almost hit and could barely contain the victory with 141⁄2-131⁄2.
“You keep reminding yourself that we had a big lead on “Valderrama”; we had a big lead on the “Brookline”, where we lost, and at “Valderrama” we won, but very difficult,” Björn said, after Europe’s 2018 team won with 5–3 sessions on Friday and yesterday, leading by 10–6 of “Le Golf National:”.
So history has to show both me and everyone in the team that it's not over.
Be prepared for an attack tomorrow.
Go out and do what you have to do.
Nothing's done until you've seen the final result on the dashboard.
We've goal and it's to try to win this trophy and that's exactly what we need to stay focused on.
“I’ve been thinking about just the 12 players on our team, but we’re also aware of our rivals who are some of the greatest players in the world.”
Satisfied with the performance of the players on a difficult golf course, Björn added: "I would never hasten the celebrations.
It'll be different tomorrow.
Tomorrow the individual performances will come to the fore, and this is different.
It's great to be there with a partner when everything is going well, but when you go out on your own, then you're putting to the test your entire capacity as a golfer.
That's the message he's supposed to get to the players: tomorrow you have to do your best.
Now you can turn your back on your partner, as he also has to deal to the end.”
Unlike Björn, rival captain Jim Furik will ask his players to perform better individually than as partners, with exceptions only Jordan Speeth and Justin Thomas, who took three out of four possible points.
Furik himself was on both sides of those big twists on the last day – he was part of the “Brookline” triumph, and then it’s on the losing side when the Europeans carried out the “Miracle of Medinah.”
“I remember every damn word,” he said in response to the question of Ben Krenschau, the captain in 1999, leading his players on the last day.
“Tomorrow we have 12 important matches, but it would be good to make the same good start as you saw on ‘Brookline’ as well as ‘Medinah.’
When the advantage goes from one to the other direction, these intermediate matches are played under enormous pressure.
That’s why we formed our squad and distributed our boys the way we want, to try to do the impossible tomorrow.”
Thomas was tasked with leading our counter-attack and confronting Rory McIlroy in the most important match, while Paul Casey, Justin Rose, John Ram, Tommy Flitwood and Jan Polter will meet the other Europeans in the top half of the arrangement.
“I have chosen these players in this order because I think it gives us the best chances of success,” Bjorn said of his selection for the singles.
Germany again delays launch of new warship
Germany's latest frigate was due to be commissioned in 2014 to replace outdated Cold War-era warships, but this will not take place until at least next year due to malfunctioning systems and rapidly rising costs, local media reported.
The commissioning of Rheinland-Pfalz, the brand's flagship of new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates, has been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to the Die Zeit newspaper, which cited an army spokesman.
The vessel was supposed to join the navy in 2014, but the problems that arose after its delivery affected the fate of the ambitious project.
The four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships, commissioned by the fleet in 2007, will replace outdated Bremen-class frigates.
It is understandable that they will have a powerful cannon, a set of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some stealth technologies, e.g. reduced radar, infrared and acoustic signals.
Other important features include longer maintenance periods – the latest frigates should be able to be used for up to two years before returning to the ports of residence.
Continuous delays, however, mean that avant-garde warships – rumoured to allow Germany to demonstrate strength overseas – will now be old when they come into service.
The ill-fought frigate F125 made headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to put the vessel into service and returned it to the Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It's the first time the navy has returned a ship to a shipbuilder after delivery.
There is little known about the reasons for the return, but the German media cited a number of significant “software and hardware defects” that make the warship unusable if it is sent on a combat mission.
Software flaws are especially important, as Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors – only half the crew of the older Bremen-class frigates.
In addition, it turned out that the ship was significantly overloaded, which reduced its efficiency and limited the ability of the navy to add future upgrades.
The 7000-tone Rheinland-Pfalz is believed to be twice as heavier as ships of a similar class used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to defective hardware, the cost of the entire project – including for crew training – is also becoming a problem.
It is estimated to have reached a staggering €3.1 billion ($3.6 billion) – of an initial €2.2 billion.
Problems with the latest frigates are becoming especially important in light of recent warnings that Germany's navy is shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartells, head of the German parliament’s defence committee, acknowledged that the navy was actually “depleting itself from ships capable of carrying out missions.”
The official said the problem had become greater in time because the old ships were decommissioned, but no new ones had been made available.
He complained that no Baden-Wuerttemberg class frigate could get involved in the fleet.
The National Trust monitors the secret life of bats
A new study carried out into a mansion in the Scottish Highlands aims to establish how bats use the landscape when hunting.
It's hoped that the findings will shed new light on the behavior of unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation actions.
The study by scientists at the National Trust of Scotland will follow common bats as well as brown arrow-eared water nightgowns at Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recording devices will be placed in key locations on the mansion to track the activities of bats during the season.
NHS employees and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using manual detectors.
The expert sound analysis of all recordings will establish the frequency of call sounds of bats, as well as which types make them.
After that, a map of the habitat and a report will be prepared to create a detailed landscape picture of their behavior.
Rob Deward, nature conservation adviser for NTS, hopes the results will reveal which areas of the habitat are most important to bats and how they are used by each species.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management work, e.g. creating meadows, as well as the best way to maintain forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bat populations in Scotland and the UK as a whole have declined significantly over the last century.
They are threatened by the construction activities that affect their shelters and lead to habitat loss.
There may be risk to populations from wind turbines and lighting, aircraft and some chemical treatments of building materials, as well as attacks from domestic cats.
In fact, bats are not blind.
However, due to their hunting habits at night, their ears are more useful than the eyes when it comes to capturing prey.
They use an advanced echolocation technique to detect insects and obstacles on their flying trajectory.
NTS, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 gardens of great importance and 76,000 hectares of land in the country, takes bats very seriously.
The trust has trained experts who conduct regular surveys, shelter inspections and sometimes rescue operations.
The organisation has even created Scotland's first and only dedicated bat reserve at the Threave estate in the Dumfries and Gallowy area, which is home to eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
The manager of the estate, David Thompson, says it's an ideal territory for them.
“Here in Threave, we have great terrain for the bats,” he noted.
“We have old buildings, many adult trees and a finished suitable habitat.
But there’s a lot more about bats that aren’t known, so the work we do here and in other properties will help us better understand what they need to thrive.”
He emphasizes that it is important to check for the presence of bats before property maintenance activities are carried out, since it is possible the involuntary destruction of an asylum to kill up to 400 female and young bats, as well as to destroy an entire local population.
Bats are a protected species and it is illegal to kill, harass, disturb or destroy their shelters.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish employee at the Bat Conservation Trust, encouraged the public to help.
She said, “I still have a lot to learn about our bats and about many of our animal species, since we just don’t know what’s happening to their populations.”
Ronaldo rejects allegations of rape and his lawyers will sue a German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo described the allegations of rape against him as "fake news," saying people "wanted to become famous" using his name.
His lawyers will sue the German news magazine Der Spiegel, which published these allegations.
Portugal and Juventus striker was accused of raping U.S. Kathryn Mayorga in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
She is said to have then paid her $375,000 to keep the incident silent, Der Spiegel reported Friday.
In an Instagram video addressing his 142 million followers, a few hours after the allegations were posted, 39-year-old Ronaldo denounced the post as “fake news.”
“No, no, no, no, no.
What they said about me today is fake news," the five-time Ballon d'Or winner told the camera.
“They want to become famous using my name.
That's normal.
They want to gain popularity on my back, but that's part of the job.
“I’m a happy person and it’s okay,” the footballer smiled.
Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel for allegations they call “inadmissible publication of privacy suspicions,” according to Reuters.
Lawyer Christian Schertz said the footballer would seek compensation for "moral damages in an amount corresponding to the severity of the violation, which is probably one of the most serious violations of personal rights in recent years."
The incident in question is said to have taken place in June 2009 in an apartment at Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga went to the footballer's room where he allegedly raped her anally, according to documents filed at Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga claims that after the incident, Ronaldo fell to his knees and told her that he was a “good boy” of “99 percent”, but in this case he simply succumbed to “one percent”.
According to the documents, Ronaldo confirmed that they had sex, but this happened with her consent.
Mayorga also claimed she had gone to the police and photos of her injuries had been taken in a hospital, but later agreed to an out-of-court settlement because she felt “terrified of retaliation” and was worried about “being publicly humiliated.”
The 34-year-old woman says she's being willing to cancel the agreement as she continues to live with the trauma of the incident in question.
At the time of the incident, Ronaldo was on the verge of joining Real Madrid from Manchester United, and this summer he moved to the Italian giants Juventus for the amount of €100 million.
Brexit: Britain 'will regret forever' losing carmakers
Britain “will regret forever” if it loses its status as a world leader in car production after Brexit, Business Secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that it was "worrying" that Toyota UK had told the BBC that if Britain left the EU without a deal, it would temporarily halt production at its plant in Bournastone, near Derby.
“We need a deal,” he said.
Japan's carmaker has said the impact of delaying the border in the event of a no-deal Brexit could lead to job losses.
Nearly 150 000 cars were produced at the Burnaston plant in Burnaston – where the Auris and Avensis models are produced last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the countries of the European Union.
“My view is that if the UK leaves the EU at the end of March, production at our plant will stop,” said Marvin Cook, Toyota’s director at Bournaston.
Other UK carmakers, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have voiced their concerns about leaving the EU without an agreement and about how cross-border trade will work.
BMW, for example, says it will close its factory for the Mini model in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The main concerns of carmakers are related to the risks to the chain of delivery in cases of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota’s production line operates on an “exacitely timely” basis, with parts arriving every 37 minutes from suppliers from both the UK and the EU for custom-made cars.
If Britain leaves the EU without a deal on March 29, there could be problems at the border, which the industry says could lead to delays and parts shortages.
It would be impossible for Toyota to wait more than a day to replenish its inventory at the Derbyshire plant, the company said, and therefore production will stop.
Mr Clark said Theresa May's "chess" plan for a future relationship with the EU was "precisely calibrated to avoid these checks at the border."
“We have to have a deal. We want to make the best deal that will allow us, as I say, not only to enjoy current success, but also to be able to grab this opportunity,” he said on BBC Radio’s 4’s Today programme.
“Following claims not only by Toyota, but by other manufacturers, we are absolutely convinced that we need to be able to continue providing successful supply chains.”
Toyota can't say how long production will be halted, but in the longer term it warns that additional costs will reduce the plant's competitiveness and ultimately lead to job losses.
Peter Tsuvalaris, who has worked in Bournaston for 24 years and is the founder of the Unite union at the plant, said its members are increasingly worried: “From my experience, I judge that if these jobs are gone, they will never come back.
A government spokesman said: “We have laid out an accurate and credible plan for our future relationship with the EU.”
Trump-Rosenstein meeting could be postponed again, White House says
An extremely important meeting between Donald Trump with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be “postponed by another week” as the fight of Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh continues, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of special adviser Robert Mueller, who is investigating Russian interference in the election, ties between Trump's aides and Russia, and a potential obstruction of justice by the president.
Whether Trump will fire the deputy attorney general or not, and thus threaten Muller's independence, has for months fueled rumours about Washington.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein was discussing the possibility of wearing a microphone to record talks with Trump, as well as the possibility of removing the president through the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein denies this claim.
But last Monday he went to the White House after reports that he would resign.
Instead, while Trump was at the United Nations headquarters in New York, a meeting was announced with him for Thursday.
Trump said he “preferented not” firing Rosenstein, but the meeting was then postponed to avoid conflict with the Senate Judgment Committee hearing that both Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual misconduct — Dr. Christine Blasey Ford — testified.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week-long investigation by the FBI of complaints against Kavano, further delaying a full Senate vote.
Trump spokeswoman, Sarah Sanders, appeared Sunday on Fox News.
When she was asked a question about the meeting with Rosenstein, she replied: “A date for this has not yet been set, it may also be this week, but seeing all the other things that are happening in the Supreme Court, in my opinion it may be postponed until next week.
But we’ll see, and I’m always trying to keep the press informed.”
Some reporters would dispute that claim: Sanders hasn't held a press conference from the White House since September 10.
The show's presenter Chris Wallace asked about the reason.
Sanders retorted that rare press conferences are not due to dislike of the “demonstrative behavior” of television journalists, although he shared: “I can’t say that I don’t agree with the fact that they have demonstrative behavior.”
She then hinted that direct contact between Trump and the press would increase.
“The president conducts more question-and-answer sessions than any president before him,” she said, adding, without citing evidence: “We’ve checked these statistics.”
There will still be press conferences, Sanders continued, but “if press officials have a chance to ask questions directly to the U.S. president, it’s far better than talking to me.
“We’re trying to do it often, and you’ve seen us practicing it quite often in recent weeks, and it’s going to replace press conferences once you’ve got a chance to talk to the President of the United States.”
Trump regularly answers questions when he leaves the White House or participates in open meetings or press conferences with visiting senior officials.
Solo press conferences are rare.
This week in New York, the president may have shown why, with his eccentric and at times bizarrely behavior in front of the assembled journalists.
The health minister has appealed to EU workers in Scotland's National Health Service regarding Brexit-related fears
The Health Secretary turned in writing to EU staff working in Scotland's National Health Service to express the country's appreciation and the desire for them to remain in places after Brexit.
Gene Freeman, a member of the Scottish Parliament, has sent a letter with less than six months remaining until Britain's exit from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already committed to the costs of established procedures for the status of EU citizens working in decentralised public services.
In her letter, Freeman wrote: “In the summer, negotiations between the UK and the EU on leaving continued, with decisions awaited this autumn.
But Britain's government is also advancing in its preparations for a possible no-deal scenario.
I'm sure it's a very uncertain period for all of you.
That's why I want to remind you how much I appreciate the contribution of each employee, regardless of his nationality.
Colleagues from all over the EU and beyond contribute with valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of health services, and this is beneficial for the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is definitely your home and we would very much like you to stay here.”
Christian Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery after suffering a head injury
Tennessee State Tigers' linebaker, Christian Abercrombie, underwent emergency surgery after suffering a head injury during Saturday's meeting, when his team lost to Vanderbilt Commodores by 31-27, Mike Organ of Tennessee reported.
Tennessee Head Coach State, Rod Reid, shared with reporters that the injury happened shortly before half-time.
“He went to the sideline and somehow he just fainted there,” Reid said.
Coaches and medical examiners handed Abercrombie oxygen to the sideline before he was laid on a stretcher and taken to the changing rooms for an additional check-up.
Tennessee State's official confirmed to Kris Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee, that Abercrombie had come out of surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Haris added that "there are still no details about the type/seriousness of the injury" and that Tennessee State is trying to figure out when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a sophomore with a college scholarship, is for the first season at Tennessee State after his transfer from Illinois.
On Saturday, he made a total of five blocks before leaving the game, making a total of 18 blocks for him this season.
Foreign buyers will be charged a higher stamp duty when buying real estate in the UK
According to new plans of the Conservatives, foreign buyers will be charged a higher stamp levy when buying real estate in Britain, with the additional funds to help the homeless.
This move will neutralize the success of Corbyn's efforts to attract young voters
The increase in the coat of arms levy will affect those who do not pay taxes in Britain
The treasury expects an increase of more than £120 million a year, which will help the homeless
Theresa May will announce today the decision for foreign buyers to pay a higher stamp levy when buying real estate in the UK, with the additional proceeds going to help homeless people.
The move will be seen as an attempt to neutralize the success of Jeremy Corbyn's efforts to attract young voters by promising more affordable housing and targeting high-income people.
The increase in the stamp duty will affect individuals and businesses that do not pay taxes in the UK, with additional revenues supporting the government's efforts to limit the number of people sleeping on the street.
The supplement – which is an addition to the current stamp duty, including the higher levels introduced two years ago for second homes and properties purchased for the purpose of rental – can amount to up to three percent.
The Treasury expects receipts to rise by up to £120million a year.
About 13 percent of London's newly built buildings are purchased by people with citizenship outside the UK, prompting an increase in prices and making it difficult for people who buy property for the first time to intervene in the property market.
Many of the rich areas – especially in the capital – have become “ghost cities” due to the large number of foreign buyers who spend most of the time outside the country.
The new policy emerges just weeks after Boris Johnson demanded a reduction in the stamp levy to help more young people own their first home.
He accused large construction companies of maintaining high property prices by plundering land, but not using it, and asked Ms May to abandon affordable housing quotas to repair Britain's "disgrace to housing."
Mr Corbyn announced an attractive series of proposals for housing reforms, including rental control and an end to “without guilt” evictions.
He is also keen to give advice greater powers to build new homes.
Mrs May said: "Last year I shared that I would like to dedicate my time as prime minister to restore the British dream - the life of every new generation to be better.
And that means repairing our shattered housing market.
Britain will always have open doors for those who wish to live, work and build their lives here.
But it is not right for individuals who do not live in Britain, as well as foreign companies, to buy homes as easily as hard-working residents of Britain.
For too many people, the dream of owning their own home has drifted too far away over time, and the insult of sleeping on the street remains too realistic.”
Jack Ross: "My ultimate goal is to lead the Scotland team"
Sunderland manager Jack Ross said the “biggest ambition” of his career was to be the head coach of Scotland’s national team.
The 42-year-old Scot accepted the challenge of reviving the club from north-eastern England, who are currently third in the First League, three points clear of the top spot.
He switched to Stadium of Light this summer after last season took Saint Mirren back to the Scottish Premier League.
“I wanted to play for my country as a player.
“I’ve scored one match for the B team and that’s it,” Ross told BBC Sportsound.
"But I grew up with the Hempden Scotland games, which as a child we watched together with my father, so the national team has always attracted me.
“However, this opportunity would only open up if I had success at club level.”
Among Ross' predecessors as Sunderland's executives are names such as Dick Advocate, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paolo Di Canio.
Aloa Athletic's former mentor said he had no anxiety as a successor to such established names at such an illustrious club, having previously rejected offers from Barnzley and Ipswich Town.
“For me, success at the moment will be measurable with a positive answer to the question “Can I bring this club back to the Premier League?”
Because in terms of structure, base and conditions he is undoubtedly at the level of the Premier League," he said.
“It won’t be easy to get back to the highest level of football, but I’m going to accept that I’ve only achieved success here if I manage to get the team out there again.”
Ros has only been a manager for three years, having spent some time as an assistant at Dumbarton and for 15 months at Hart's coaching staff.
He then helped Aloa not fall into the Third Division and turned St Mirren from relegating team into a Championship winner next season.
Ross says he now feels more comfortable than during his career as a footballer in Clyde, Hartlipool, Falkirk, St. Mirren and the Hamilton Academic.
“I was really at a crossroads,” he recalled of the time he took Aloa.
“I really believed that being a manager was the best field for me, including being an active player.
It sounds strange because I was doing well, I was making good money and I had some significant successes.
But being a player sometimes is hard.
There's a lot you have to deal with every week.
I'm still facing the same levels of stress and tension at work, but I'm just feeling that being a manager is the right direction for me.
I’ve always wanted to be a manager, and now that I’ve really done it, I’ve never felt more comfortable in my skin throughout my conscious life
You can hear the full interview on Sportsound on Sunday, September 30, on Radio Scotland frequencies between 12:00 and 13:00 British summer time
The ideal time for beer is 5:30pm on Saturday, research reveals
The summer heat has boosted revenues of struggling British pubs, but racked up more tensions for restaurant chains.
Pubs and bar groups reported an increase in sales by 2.7 percent in July, but revenue in restaurants fell by 4.8 percent, statistics show.
Peter Martin of the business-consultant CGA, which compiled the statistics, said: "Due to prolonged sunny weather and longer than England's expected participation in the World Cup, in July follows a similar model as June, when pubs grew by 2.8 percent, but restaurants received an even stronger blow.
The 1.8 percent drop in the restaurant business in June only deepened in July.
Pubs and beverage bars have reported the best results in their growth, compared to the downward movement of restaurants.
Pubs with meals also suffered from the sunny weather, but not as much as restaurant owners.
It looks like people just wanted to go out and have a drink.
In pubs and bars, beverage sales jumped 6.6 percent for the month, while there was a 3 percent reduction in food.”
Paul Newman, of recreation and entertainment analysts RSM, said: “These results continue the trend that we noticed at the end of April.
The time and influence of major social or sporting events remain the biggest factors when it comes to sales in the service sector outside the home.
It's no surprise that restaurant groups continue to suffer, although a 4.8 percent year-on-year drop in sales may be particularly painful in addition to fixed costs.
The long hot summer couldn’t have appeared at a worse time for the managers of the food restaurants, and only time will tell if the more moderate temperatures we experienced in August will bring the much-desired holiday.”
The total growth in sales in pubs and restaurants, including the newly opened ones, was 2.7 percent in July, reflecting a slowdown in brand rollouts.
Coffer Peach Tracker, the UK's pubs, bar and restaurant sales analyst, collected and analysed performance data from 47 operational groups, with a total turnover of more than £9 billion, and was an established benchmark in the industry.
One in five children hides from their parents a secret profile on a social network
One in five children – some only 11 – has a secret profile on a social network that it hides from its parents and teachers, a study has revealed
A survey conducted among 20,000 middle school students showed a rise in fake Insta pages
The news has raised fears that material with sexual content is being published
Twenty percent of students said they had a “basic” profile they showed their parents
One in five children – some one in 11 – creates a profile on a social network that is kept secret from adults.
A survey of 20,000 middle school students has shown a rise in fake Insta accounts – a reference to the photo-sharing site Instagram.
The news has raised fears that material with sexual content is being published.
Twenty percent of students said they had a clear “basic” profile that they showed their parents, while also having secret profiles.
A mother who stumbled upon the 13-year-old daughter’s secret website found a teenage child who encouraged others to “rape him.”
The survey, conducted by Digital Awareness UK and Independent Schools' Conference of Directors (HMC), found that 40 percent of children between the ages of 11 and 18 had two profiles, with half admitting they kept their accounts a secret.
HMC supervisor Mike Buchanan said: “It’s worrying that so many teenagers are tempted to create online spaces where parents and teachers can’t find them.”
Eilid Doyle will be the ‘voice of the athletes’ at the Board of Athletics in Scotland
Eilid Doyle was chosen to join the Board of Athletics in Scotland as non-executive director at the ruling body's annual general meeting.
Doyle is Scotland's athlete with the most achievements and President Iain Beattie has described the move as a great opportunity for those leading in the sport to take advantage of her extensive experience at the international level over the past decade.
“Eilid enjoys tremendous respect in the athletic communities of Scotland, the UK and the world, and we are convinced that athletics in Scotland will benefit greatly by attracting it,” Beatty said.
Doyle said: "I want to be the voice of the athletes and I hope I can really contribute and help promote the sport in Scotland."
The American, who won the finals of the 200 and 400 meters at the 1996 Atlanta Olympic Games, adding to a total of his four Olympic gold medals, and is currently a regular commentator at the BBC, has lost the ability to walk after suffering a transient ischaemic seizure.
He tweeted: “A month ago I had a stroke.
I couldn't walk.
Doctors said that only time will tell whether I will recover and to what extent.
It was an exhausting job, but I achieved a full recovery, I learned to go again and today I do workouts for motility!
Thank you for the encouraging messages!”
Advertising for a breast pump, which compares mothers with cows, divided online reviews
A company producing breast pumps has divided online opinions with an advertisement that compares nursing mothers with cows getting milked.
In order to mark the launch of the expected “first-silent milk pump in the world suitable for wearing”, consumer technology company Elvie has launched a joking music-video advertisement to demonstrate the freedom that the new pump provides nursing mothers to express themselves.
Four real moms dance in a cow barn full of hay, amid a song with a lyric including: “Yes, I’m milking, but you don’t see any queue” and “If you haven’t noticed, it’s not udder, it’s my breasts.”
The chorus continues: “Pump, pump, feed the babies with them, pump, pump, nurse your girlfriends.”
However, advertising posted on the company's Facebook page caused online disagreements.
With 77 000 views and hundreds of comments, the video received mixed reactions from viewers, with many saying it downplayed the “horrors” of the dairy industry.
“It’s a very stupid decision to use cows to advertise this product.
Like us, they have to get pregnant and give birth in order to produce milk, but their babies take them a few days after birth," someone wrote.
The Elvie breast pump is collected discreetly in a nursing bra (Elvie/Mother)
“It’s perfectly understandable that it’s traumatizing for both the mother and the baby.
But yes, why don’t we use them to advertise a breastfeeding pump for moms who can save their babies?”
Someone else added: “Such inadequate advertising.”
Others defended the commercial, with a woman admitting she considered the song "extremely funny."
“I think it’s a genius.
I'd buy one if I was still breastfeeding.
Squeezing made me feel just like a cow.
It's a little crazy, but I take it the way it is.
“This product is genius,” someone wrote.
“It’s a funny advertisement aimed at mothers straining (often in their workplaces or toilets) and feeling like ‘cows.’
It is not an advertisement praising or condemning the dairy industry.”
At the end of the video, the group of women revealed that they had danced with the discreet pumps placed in their bras.
The concept of the campaign is based on the view that many women who breastfeed feel like cows.
The Elvie pump, however, is completely silent, has no wires or tubes, and collects discreetly in a nursing bra that gives women the freedom to move, keep their babies, and even go outside as they drain.
Anna Balarin, Partner and Executive Creative Director at Mother, commented: “The Elvie pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserves a bold and provocative launch.
By drawing a parallel between nursing mothers and dairy cows, we wanted to draw attention to expressing breast milk and all the challenges associated with this, while showing in a fun and understandable way the incredible feeling of freedom that the new pump will bring.
It's not the first time the Elvie pump has come across the headline news.
During London Fashion Week, a mother of two appeared on the podium for designer Martha Jakoubowski using the product.
Hundreds of migrant children have been secretly moved to a tent camp at the border with Texas
The number of detained children of migrants has skyrocketed, although monthly border crossings remain relatively unchanged, partly because sharp rhetoric and policy on the part of the Trump administration has made it difficult to accommodate children with guardians.
Typically, most guardians are undocumented immigrants who are afraid they are risking remaining in the country if they wish to recognize a child.
The risk increased in June when federal authorities announced that potential guardians and other adults from the family should provide fingerprints, with this data to be shared with immigration authorities.
Last week, Matthew Albens, a high-ranking Immigration and Customs Service official, testified to Congress that the agency had arrested dozens of people wishing to become guardians of unaccompanied minors.
The agency later confirmed that 70 percent of those arrested did not have previous criminal records.
“Nearly 80 percent of persons who are either guardians or members of the guardian family reside illegally in the country, with a large majority of them being foreign criminals.
So we're going to continue to prosecute these individuals," Albens said.
In an effort to deal more quickly with registering these children, the authorities have imposed new rules that require some of them to appear before the court within a month of their detention, rather than after 60 days, as was the previous norm, according to employees at the shelter.
Many appear in a video conference call, rather than personally, to present their case for legal status before an immigration judge.
Those who are deemed unsuitable for admission will be deported as soon as possible.
The longer the children remain detained, the more likely they are to become depressed or depressed, which can lead to violent outbursts or escape attempts, according to employees at the shelter and reports that have come out of the system in recent months.
Lawyers shared that this concern is growing with large facilities, such as that in Tornillo, where signs that a child has problems are more likely to be overlooked because of the scale of the place.
They added that moving the children to the “tent city” without giving them enough time to prepare on an emotional level or say goodbye to their friends can aggravate the trauma that many are already struggling with.
Syria urges “occupation troops” of the United States, France and Turkey to immediately withdraw
Addressing the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Mualem also called for Syrian refugees to return home, although the civil war has been going on for an eighth year.
Mualem, who also holds the position of deputy prime minister, said foreign troops were residing illegally on Syrian soil under the pretext of fighting terrorism and “the appropriate action will be taken towards them.”
"They should be withdrawn immediately and without any conditions," he told the meeting.
Mualem insisted that “the war on terror is almost coming to an end” in Syria, where more than 360,000 people have lost their lives since 2011, and millions are forcibly displaced from their homes.
He said Damascus would continue “to fight in this sacred battle until all Syrian lands are cleansed” by both terrorist organizations and “any illegal presence of foreigners.”
The U.S. has about 2,000 troops in Syria, which mainly trains and advises both Kurdish units and Syrians, opposition to President Bashar al-Assad.
France has more than 1,000 servicemen in the lands of the war-ravaged country.
With regard to refugees, Mualem said conditions were tolerable and could return, accusing “some Western countries of “blowing up baseless fears” prompting refugees to stay away.
“We have asked the international community and the humanitarian organizations to contribute to these returns,” he said.
“They’re turning into a political question something that’s an entirely humanitarian issue.”
The U.S. and the European Union have warned that no aid will be provided to rebuild Syria until there is a political agreement between Assad and the opposition to stop the war.
U.N. diplomats said the recent agreement between Russia and Turkey on the creation of a buffer zone in Idlib's last major rebel stronghold allowed for progress in political dialogues.
The agreement between Russia and Turkey prevented a large-scale attack by Russian-backed Syrian forces on the province, where three million people live.
Mualem stressed, however, that the agreement had “clear deadlines” and expressed hope that hostilities would target the jihadists, including fighters from the al-Qaeda-linked al-Qaida-linked al-Nusra front, which “will be destroyed.”
U.N. envoy Staffan de Mistura hopes to soon convene the first meetings of the new committee, made up of members of the government and the opposition, to form a draft of Syria's post-war constitution and pave the way for elections.
Muallem laid out the Syrian government’s conditions for participation in the committee, saying the panel’s work should be limited to a “review of articles of the current constitution” and warned there should be no interference.
Why would Trump win a second term
By that logic, Trump will win re-election in the 2020 election, unless – as many viewers from the Liberals probably hope – his presidency is not terminated prematurely because of impeachment and scandal.
It would no doubt be “the most dramatic end to a presidency ever!”
At the moment there are no signs of spectator fatigue.
Since 2014, primetime ratings have doubled to 1.05 million on CNN and nearly three-fold to 1.6 million for MSNBC.
Fox News had an average of 2.4 million viewers during primetime, up from 1.7 million four years ago, according to Nielsen, and MSNBC's "The Rachel Maddow Show" was at the top at cable TV ratings, with up to 3.5 million viewers at big-news evenings.
“It’s a fire that attracts people because it’s something we don’t understand,” said Neil Baer, executive producer of ABC’s “Last Candidate” drama, which is told of a Cabinet secretary who became president after an attack on Capitol Hill.
Nell Scovell, a veteran comedy screenwriter and the author of “Just the Funny Parts: And a Few Hard Truths About Sneaking Into the Hollywood Boys’ Club,” has another theory.
She remembers a ride in a taxi in Boston ahead of the 2016 election.
The taxi driver told her he was going to vote for Mr. Trump.
She asked, “Why?”
Ms. Skovel told me that he replied to her, “Because it makes me laugh.”
There's fun in chaos.
Of course, in contrast to everything else that's being shown on television, stories coming out of Washington can determine the future of "Roe v. Wade," whether immigrant families can reunite, as well as the good fortunes of the global economy.
Exclusion is a luxury that only the most privileged viewers can afford.
Still, the desire to be an informed citizen doesn’t match this at six o’clock to watch a group of experts discuss Bob Woodward, who uses “deep historical information” as a source for his book “Fear”; Paul Manafort’s 15,000 £15,000 (“Arrogance-filled outfit” according to “Washington Post”), and Staggering descriptions of Stormy Daniel.
I'll never look at Super Mario the same way again.
“Part of what he’s doing to make you feel like part of a reality show is giving you a little bit every night,” said Brent Montgomery, chief executive producer of Wheelhouse Entertainment and Pawn Stars creator, in terms of shifting contestants and day-to-day twists (nagging the National Football League praising Kim Jong Un).
You can't afford to miss an episode because you're going to fall behind.
When I contacted Mr. Fleiss this week, it was sunny for him on the north coast of Kauai, 80 degrees, but he had shut himself down at home to watch MSNBC while recording CNN.
He couldn't separate himself from the TV, especially when Brett Kavanaugh had to stand up in front of the Senate Judgment's Judgment, and that's what the future of the Supreme Court depended on.
“I remember when we used to do all those crazy shows and people used to say, ‘It’s the beginning of the end for Western civilization,’” Fleice told me.
“I thought it was a joke, but it turned out that they were right.”
Amy Chosik, a freelance journalist for The Times in the fields of business, politics and media, is the author of Chasing Hillary.
Investments are flowing into the most contested race in the early congressional election
Unsurprisingly, Pennsylvania's 17th county receives a flow of cash, thanks to the rearrangement of congressional counties, and hence two holders for the same place.
Pittsburgh's recently redistributed county points to Conservative Democrat Connor Lamb, who won his seat from another county in a partial election last spring.
Lambe is up against another titulator, Republican Keith Rothfuss, who currently represents the old 12th district of Pennsylvania, which significantly advocates with the new 17th.
The maps were re-drawn after the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ordered in January that old counties were unconstitutionally manipulated in favor of Republicans.
The race in the new 17th county sparked a financial dispute between the party's main funding institutions, the Democratic Campaigns Committee (DCCC) and the National Republican Campaigns Committee (NRCC).
Lambus became famous in Pennsylvania after the hard-fought victory in the widely debated partial elections in March for the 18th Pennsylvania Convention District.
The position was held by Republicans for more than a decade, and President Donald Trump won the county by 20 points.
Political experts believe the Democrats have a slight advantage.
The U.S. has considered imposing sanctions on El Salvador over its support for China, then withdrew
The diplomats noted that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already recognized Beijing, with little pressure from Washington.
Mr. Trump had a cordial meeting with President Juan Carlos Varella from Panama in June 2017, and also owned a hotel in Panama until the partners fired the management team from the Trump Organization.
State Department officials have decided to recall the heads of diplomatic missions in El Salvador, Dominican Republic and Panama, due to "recentive decisions to no longer recognise Taiwan," Heather Nauert, a spokeswoman for the Department, said earlier this month.
But sanctions were foreseen only against El Salvador, who in 2017 received about $140 million in aid from America for the fight against drugs, for the purpose of development and economic assistance.
The proposed sanctions, including cuts to financial aid and targeted visa restrictions, would have been harrowing for the Central American state with its unemployment and homicide rates.
As internal meetings advanced, officials from North and Central America have postponed a high-level conference aimed at security and economic well-being, which served as a follow-up to a similar gathering last year seen as a step forward in an effort to avert the influx of migrants to the U.S.
But by mid-September, senior governing bodies had clarified that their wish was for the conference to take place, thus effectively ending any discussion of sanctions against El Salvador.
Vice President Mike Pence has now announced that he will participate in a conference planned for mid-October as a sign of the importance the administration attaches to the gathering, diplomats say.
All three U.S. envoys have quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic without new strict announcements or sanctions from Washington.
A White House spokesman for Mr. Bolton declined to comment on the details of the debate described by three U.S. officials, including two diplomats wishing to remain anonymous, who agreed to participate in the internal deliberations.
Their narratives were confirmed by an external analyst close to the administration who also wished to remain anonymous.
History of the study
The next move could be Special Adviser Robert Mueller's report on possible obstruction of justice by Mr. Trump, with the reporting currently significant evidence in the public register.
Mr. Mueller is said to be directing his investigation into whether Mr. Trump conspired with Russia during his campaign in their attack on our elections.
If the congressional leadership changes, it will turn out that Mr. Trump will have to be accountable to that authority, just as he is preparing to face voters, as well as, perhaps, to jurors made up of his colleagues.
There are a lot of unknowns here, and I’m not suggesting that Mr. Trump’s downfall is inevitable—nor that of those like him in Europe.
All of us, on both sides of the Atlantic, have to make these choices that will affect how much the fight will extend.
In 1938, German officers were ready to carry out a coup d'état against Hitler as long as the West had confronted him and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed and missed the opportunity to avoid the ensuing years of massacres.
The course of history depends on such nodular moments, and the inexorable march of democracy speeds up or slows down.
At the moment, Americans are facing several similar nodules.
What will we do if Mr. Trump fires Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who holds the fate of Mr. Mueller's investigation in his hands?
Rosenstein has been in trouble ever since this newspaper reported that last year he had offered to secretly record the president and had speculated on the view that he was not fit for office.
Mr. Rosenstein announced that the story in The Times was inaccurate.
“How are we going to respond if the new investigation against Brett Kavanaugh demanded by the FBI isn’t complete or fair – or if it’s approved for the Supreme Court despite allegations of sexual assault and perjury?”
And, above all, are we going to vote in the early congressional elections if it's forcing Mr. Trump to take responsibility?
If we fail these trials, democracy is waiting for a long winter.
But I believe we won't fail because I've learned from Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovakia Jewish woman who was taken to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that had once occupied my ambassadorial home.
She survived, immigrated to America, and 60 years later sent me to light the Shabbat candles at that table wearing a swastika.
With a legacy like this, how can I not be optimistic about our future?”
Norman Eisen, a senior fellow at the Brookings Institution, is chairman of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, D.C., and is the author of "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
Ranger's Graham Dorans is optimistic about the clash with Rapid Vienna
Rangers host Rapid Vienna on Thursday, being aware that victory over the Austrians after Spain's impressive draw against Villarreal earlier this month will give them a significant chance to move forward from Group G of the Europa League.
Knee injury did not allow midfielder Graham Dorans to play his first game of the season ahead of the 2-2 draw with Villarreal in question, but he believes for Rangers this result could become a springboard to greater achievements.
“We’ve won an important point because Villarreal are a good opponent,” the 31-year-old said.
“We entered the match with the hope that we can win something and we left with a point.
Maybe we could've even won at the end of the match, but overall the draw seems a fair result.
They were better in the first half and we were in the second half.
We've taken a look at Thursday, another big night of our matches in Europe.
I hope that we will be able to fight for three points, this will be a difficult match, because they achieved a good result in the last meeting, but I am sure that with the support of our audience we can give everything and achieve a good result.
Last year it was definitely difficult after everything that happened past my injuries and the changes at the club, but now things seem to have worked out in the rule direction.
The team is good and the boys are really having fun, the training is fruitful.
I hope that we will be able to harness all our strength to forget last season and achieve the desired success.”
Women are worried over fears about their retirement savings
Despite the fact that the participants in the study had a clear idea of what worries they wanted to receive, few people discussed this with family members.
About half of the participants in a Nationwide study said they had discussed with their spouses the costs of long-term care.
Only 10 percent have spoken to their children about it.
“People want a family member to take care of them, but they haven’t taken the steps towards having that conversation,” said Holly Snyder, vice president of Nationwide’s life insurance sector.
Here's what you can start with.
Speak with your spouse and children: You can’t prepare your family to provide you with care if you don’t state your wishes in advance.
Together with your counselor and your family, discuss where and how you want are receiving care, as these solutions can be a crucial factor in determining costs.
Include your financial advisor: Your advisor can also help you with an idea of how to pay those costs.
Your decisions when choosing a long-term care financial institution may include a traditional long-term life insurance policy with monetary value to help yourself cover those costs or self-insurance with your own funds – if you have the cash resources.
Draw up your legal documents: Deal with the legal battles from the very beginning.
Appoint a health care proxy so that you have a designated confidante to oversee your medical care and ensure that professionals comply with your wishes in case you cannot communicate.
Consider also the idea of a proxy in financial matters.
You can choose a trusted person to make financial decisions on your behalf and ensure that your bills will be paid if you are incapacitated.
Don’t forget the small details: Imagine that your elderly parent needs urgent medical attention and travels to the hospital.
Will you be able to answer questions about medications and allergies?
Expose these details in writing so that you are prepared.
“It’s not just about the financial aspect, it’s also about who the doctors will be,” Martin asks.
“What are the drugs?
Who will take care of the dog?
Draw up a plan.”
A man was shot several times with an air rifle in Ilfracomb
A man was shot several times with an air rifle as he drove home in the evening outside.
The victim, around the age of 40, was stationed in the Oxford Grove area of Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was shot in the chest, stomach and arm.
Officers described the shooting, which occurred around 02:30 a.m
The victim was unable to see his attacker.
His injuries are not life-threatening and police are trying to attract witnesses.
Earthquakes and tsunami in Indonesia
At least 384 people have died from a powerful earthquake and tsunami that struck the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, authorities said, with the death toll expected to rise.
Due to disrupted communications, support organizations could not obtain information from Regency Dongala, an area north of Palu that is located closer to the epicenter of the 7.5-magnitude quake.
More than 16 000 people were evacuated in Palu after the disaster.
Here are some basic facts about Palu and Dongala, which are located on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of the province of central Sulawesi, located at the end of a narrow beach on the west coast of Sulawesi island, with a population of 379,800 people in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the quake and tsunami hit it.
Dongala is a regency that stretches more than 300km (180 miles) along the coastline of Sulawesi Island.
The regency, which an administrative region smaller than a province, has an estimated population of 299 200 in 2017.
Fishing and farming are the foundations of the economy of the province of central Sulawesi, especially in the coastal region of Dongala.
Nickel production is also important for the province, but it is concentrated mainly in Morovali, on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Dongala have been hit several times by a tsunami over the past 100 years, according to data from the Indonesian Disaster Recovery Agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of buildings in Dongala.
Tsunami hit the western Dongala and in 1996, when nine people were killed.
Indonesia is located on the seismic “Pacific Ring of Fire” and is regularly struck by earthquakes.
Here are some of the big tsunami earthquakes and tsunami in recent years:
2004: A strong quake on December 26 on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Azekh in North Sumatra activated a tsunami that overwhelmed 14 countries and killed 226,000 people along the entire coastline of the Indian Ocean, with more than half of them in Aceh.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes hit the west coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds died on the island of Nias off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude quake struck the most populous island in Indonesia, Java, activating a tsunami that crashed into the south coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: 7.6-magnitude Earthquake strikes near the town of Padang, the West Sumatra province's capital.
More than 1100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude quake struck one of the Mentavai islands and activated a 10-foot tsunami wave that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A shallow quake struck Regency Pidi Jaya in Atsekh, causing destruction and panic as people remembred the devastations of the deadly earthquake and tsunami in 2004.
A tsunami was not activated this time, but more than 100 people were killed by collapsed buildings.
2018: Severe quakes struck the tourist island of Lombok in Indonesia, more than 500 people died, most of them on the northern side of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists temporarily at an impasse.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested on domestic violence charges
Trak Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska governor and vice-presidential candidate Sarah Palin, has been arrested on charges of violence.
Palin, a 29-year-old, from Wasila, Alaska, was arrested on suspicion of domestic violence, interfering with a notice of domestic violence and resisting arrest, according to an Alaska state police report released on Saturday.
According to the police report, when a female acquaintance tried to call the police to report the alleged crimes, he took away her phone.
Palin was detained in the Mat-Su pre-detention building and unsecured $500, KTUU reported.
