Federal prosecutors want a rarely death penalty for a suspect in a terrorist attack in New York
New York federal prosecutors are seeking the death penalty for Sayfulo Saipov, the suspect in the New York terror bombing, in which eight people died – a rare punishment that has not been carried out in the state for a 1953 federal crime.
Saipov, 30, is said to have used a Home Depot rental truck to carry out the bombing in October on a bike lane near the West Side Highway in lower Manhattan, gassing pedestrians and cyclists on their way.
To acquit the death sentence, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov “intentionally” killed eight victims and “intentionally” caused serious bodily harm, according to the notification of intent in connection with the request for the death penalty filed in the southern district of New York.
According to the court document, both charges can end with a death sentence.
Weeks after the attack, an enlarged federal judicial panel issued an indictment to Saipov with 22 points, including eight counts of homicide related to racketeering, commonly used by federal prosecutors in cases of organized crime, as well as a charge of violence and the destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required "serious planning and premeditation," prosecutors said, describing Saipov's manner of carrying out as "terrible, cruel and perverse."
“Saifulo Habibulayevich Saipov caused injuries, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicolás Klevez, Anne-Lori Decade, Darren Drake, Ariel Earley, Hernan Fellucci and Alejandro Damian Panuco,” it said.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It's been a decade since New York's Southern District last sought a death sentence.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was charged with the murder of two drug dealers but was subsequently sentenced to life sentence in September 2009.
The last time a death sentence was carried out in New York in a federal case was in 1953 – Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple who were executed after a conviction for conspiracy and carrying out espionage for the Soviet Union during the Cold War, two years earlier.
Both Rosenberg was executed in the electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, an Uzbekistan by nationality, showed no sense of remorse during the days and months after the attack, according to court documents.
He told investigators he felt good about what he had done, police said.
Saipov told authorities that he was inspired to carry out the attack after watching videos of ISIS on his phone, the indictment claims.
He has also demanded that an ISIS flag be placed in his hospital room, police said.
He pleaded not guilty to the indictment, consisting of 22 points.
David Patton, one of the federal public defenders representing Saipov, noted that they were “obviously disappointed” by the prosecutor’s decision.
“According to us, the decision to seek a death sentence instead of accepting a sentence of guilt and life imprisonment without the possibility of early release will only prolong the traumatizing effect of these events for everyone affected,” Patton said.
Saipov's defense team had previously asked the prosecutor's office not to seek a death sentence.
