The number of fugitives from North Korea 'declines' under Kim
A South Korean legislature says the number of fugitives from North Korea to South Korea has declined since Kim Jong-un came to power seven years ago.
Bieong Sug Park, citing data from the South Korean Unification Ministry, said there were 1127 fugitives last year, compared to 2706 in 2011.
According to Mr. Park, stricter border controls between North Korea and China, as well as the higher sums demanded by human smugglers, are the key factors.
There was no official comment from Pyongyang.
Most of the fugitives from the North ended up earning citizenship in South Korea.
Seoul says more than 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most are fleeing through China, where the longest border with North Korea is located and it is easier to cross than the heavily guarded demilitarized zone (DMZ), which divides the two Koreas.
China considers fugitives to be illegal migrants, not refugees, often forcibly repatriating them.
Relations between North and South Korea, which are technically still at war, have improved significantly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the heads of state of the two countries met in Pyongyang for talks concentrating on the stalemate in nuclear disarmament talks.
This happened after the historic meeting between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, where they have negotiated in general terms working for a Korean peninsula without nuclear weapons.
On Saturday, however, North Korea's Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho blamed U.S. sanctions for the lack of progress ever since.
“In the absence of trust in the United States, we will not be confident in our national security, and under these circumstances, there is no way to disarm ourselves first unilaterally,” Mr. Ri said in a speech to the U.N. General Assembly in New York.
