“I couldn’t haven’t thought that if a woman had reacted like this, they’d have called her ‘hysterical,’» Pelosi said of her reaction to Kavanaugh’s testimony before the Senate Judicial Commission on Thursday.
Kavano emotionally dismissed claims that he sexually assaulted Dr. Christine Blasey Ford when they were both teenagers.
During his inaugural speech, Kavanaugh was very emotional, at times almost shouting and getting emotional when discussing his family and school years.
He also strongly condemned Democrats on the committee, calling the claims against him a "grotesque and coordinated attack on the individual" organized by liberals who are unhappy that Hillary Clinton lost the 2016 presidential election.
Pelosi pointed out that, according to her, Kavano's testimony proved that he was unable to work in the Supreme Court because it had become clear that he was biased against Democrats.
“I think he’s disqualifying himself with these claims and the way he’s chasing the Clinton family and the Democrats,” she said.
Pelosi denied when asked if she would try to discredit Kavanaugh if he was appointed and in the event that Democrats gain a majority in the House of Representatives.
“I’m going to put it this way – if he doesn’t tell the truth to Congress or the FBI, then he’s unfit not only to enter the Supreme Court, but also the court he’s in now,” Pelosi said.
Kavano is currently a judge at the Court of Appeal in Washington.
Pelosi added that in her capacity as Democrat she was concerned about Kavano's potential rulings regarding the Affordable Health Care Act or the "Roe v. Wade" case because he was considered a conservative judge.
In preliminary hearings, Kavanaux avoided questions about whether he would reject certain decisions of the Supreme Court.
“Now isn’t the time for a hysterical, biased person to go into court and expect us to say, ‘isn’t it great,’” Pelosi concluded.
And women have to control themselves.
It's legitimate criticism, after months and years of dispersing rage, and she can't accept it without crying.
“We’re crying when we’re angry,” Mrs. Steinum told me 45 years later.
“I don’t find it unusual, do you?”
She continued, “I received tremendous help from a woman who was an executive somewhere, who shared that she also cried when she got angry, but had developed her own technique, thanks to which, when she got angry and crying, she told her interlocutor, “You might think I’m sad because I’m crying.
“I’m angry.”
And then it just keeps going.
“I thought it was brilliant.”
Tears are allowed by the expression of anger, partly because they are misunderstood.
One of my clearest memories of a previous job, in an office mostly with men, is how, once, while sobbing of an inexpressible rage, an elder than me a woman grabbed me by the neck – a chilling supervisor I was always a little afraid of – and she dragged me to the stairwell.
“Never let them see you crying,” she told me.
“They don’t know you’re angry.
“They think you’re sad, and they’ll be happy they’ve done it to you.”
Patricia Schroeder, at the time a democratic congressman from Colorado, worked with Gary Hart in his campaign for president.
In 1987, when Mr. Hart was caught in an extramarital affair on a yacht called “Monkey Business” and withdrew from the race, Mrs. Schroeder, deeply disappointed, decided that there was no reason not to consider the idea of running for president herself.
“It wasn’t a well-thought-out decision,” she told me 30 years later, laughing.
“There were already seven other candidates in the race, and the last thing they wanted to happen was for another one to appear.
Someone called the situation “Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs.”
As she joined the campaign later, she fell behind with fundraising and decided she wouldn't stay in the race if she couldn't raise $2 million.
The battle is previously doomed to failure.
She realized that some of her supporters, who gave £1000 to men, would give only $250 to her.
“Do they think I’m getting a discount?” she wondered.
During her speech, in which she declares that she will not continue with an official campaign, she is flooded with so many feelings – gratitude to the people who have supported her, discontent against the system, because of which it is so difficult to raise money and be able to turn to the voters, and not to the delegates, as well as anger against sexism that she became emotional.
“One might think that I had a nervous breakdown,” Mrs. Schroeder recalled how the media had reacted.
“Someone might think that Kleenex is my corporate sponsor.
I remember thinking about what they were going to write on my tombstone?
“She cried?”
How a U.S.-China trade war could serve Beijing
The first shots in the U.S.-China trade war were deafening and, while the battle is just gaining momentum, a rift between the two countries will benefit Beijing in the long run, experts say.
Donald Trump, the president of the United States, has launched the first warning salvo this year, taxing key elements of China's exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The biggest escalation of tensions emerged this week with the new tariffs hitting $200bn ($150bn) worth of effectively taxing half of all goods imported into the U.S. from China.
Each time, Beijing retaliated with counter-new tariffs with five to ten percent of U.S. $60 billion worth of U.S. goods.
China has vowed to retaliate against any U.S. strike, and soon the world's second-largest economy isn't expected to ignore that.
Getting Washington to give up means succumbing to the demands, but bowing its head publicly to Washington would be too shameful for Xi Jinping, China's president.
And yet, according to experts, if Beijing plays its cards correctly, the pressure from the trade war with the U.S. could help China in the long run with reducing the interdependency of the two economies.
“The fact that a hasty political decision, both in Washington and Beijing, could create conditions for an economic crisis in any of the two states is actually more dangerous than observers previously admitted,” said Abigail Grace, a research fellow in Asia-related issues at the Center for New U.S. Security.
Syria is “ready” for the return of refugees, foreign minister says
Syria has announced that it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and is asking for assistance in the reconstruction of the war-ravaged country, which lasted more than seven years.
In an address to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Muallem pointed out that the conditions in the country are improving.
“Today, the situation on the ground is more stable and safe, thanks to the progress we have in the fight against terrorism,” he said.
The government continues to rebuild areas devastated by terrorists to bring them back to normal.
Now there are all the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees to the state, which they were forced to leave because of terrorism and the unilateral economic measures that affected their daily lives and livelihood.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have left the state since the war began in 2011.
Another six million people who still live there are in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Mualem said the Syrian regime would welcome any assistance in restoring the devastated state.
But he stressed that no conditional assistance or assistance would be accepted from countries that financed the rebels.
Europe wins at the Ryder Cup in Paris
Team Europe won the 2018 Rider Cup, defeating the U.S. team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at Le Golf National on the outskirts of Paris, France.
Thus, the U.S. has suffered six consecutive losses on European soil and has not won the Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe regained its championship corona in the tournament after the team led by Danish captain Thomas Bjorn came to the required 14.5 points, which guaranteed victory over the United States.
U.S. star Phil Mickelson, who performed poorly throughout most of the tournament, hurled his first shot on the 16th hole (with “par” 3) in the water, losing his match against Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari performed excellently in all rounds, becoming one of the four players in the history of the tournament that reached a 5-0-0 balance since the race took place in the current format after 1979.
American Jordan Speath lost definitively 5&4 from the player in the team of Europe, located in the lowest place in the rankings – Thorbjörn Olesen from Denmark.
The leader in the world ranking Dustin Johnson lost 2 and 1 to Ian Poulter of England, for whom this is probably the last participation of the “Ryder Cup”.
Spaniard Sergio Garcia, a veteran with eight appearances in the Ryder Cup, became the European with the most points earned in the tournament of all time, this year increasing his asset to 25.5 points.
“I don’t usually cry, but I can’t resist today.
It's been a tough year.
I'm extremely grateful to Thomas for calling me and believing in me.
I'm so glad I'd pick up the bowl again.
In all the most important is the team and I am happy that I was able to help, "said the excited Garcia after the victory of Europe.
He passes the baton to his compatriot John Rahm, who beat American legend Tiger Woods with 2&1 the singles on Sunday.
“I’m incredibly proud to have beaten Tiger Woods, yet I grew up watching him play,” Ram said.
Woods has lost all four matches in France and his total Ryder Cup balance is now 13-21-3.
Weird statistics for one of the greatest players of all time, having won 14 major titles – an indicator by which is inferior only to Jack Niklaus.
The U.S. team struggled throughout the weekend, excluding Patrick Reid, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who showed high-class golf on all days of the tournament.
U.S. team captain Jim Furick shared after the team’s disappointing performance: “I’m proud of these guys, they struggled.
At one time this morning we cornered the Europe team.
There's been a fight.
I'm taking my hat off to Thomas.
He's a great captain.
All his 12 players performed very well.
We're going to regroup, we're going to discuss the important issues with the U.S. Association of Professional Golfers and our Rider Cup Committee, and we'll only look ahead.
I love these 12 guys and I'm proud to be their captain.
You can't help but take off an opponent's hat.
“We’ve just been outplayed.”
New for the red tide: Concentrations decline in Pinelas, Manotti and Sarasota
The most recent report by the Florida Fish and Wildlife Committee has shown a general reduction in concentrations of red tides in sections of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the Committee, there are reports on conditions for breakage of growth in the districts of Pinelas, Mannoty, Sar�çota, Charlotte and Collier – this indicates a decrease in concentrations.
Water sections with an excessive heyday of the red tide spread approximately 130 miles along the coast, from the northern Pinellas county to the southern county of Lee.
Algae sections can be seen 10 miles from Hillsborough County coast, but are fewer compared to last week.
A red tide was also observed in Pasco County.
Over the past week, there have been reports of moderate concentrations in the coastal area or slightly deeper into Pinellas county, low to high concentrations along Hillsborough County, preconditions for high concentrations in Mannoty County, preconditions for high concentrations on the coastline or further inside Sarasota County, preconditions for moderate concentrations in Charlotte county, prerequisites for high concentrations on the coast or further inside Lee county.
There are ongoing reports of airway inflammations in the districts of Pinellas, Mannoty, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Over the past week, there have been no reports of respiratory tract inflammation in northwest Florida.
